1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,000
Hi, I'm Kelly Monaco and welcome again to Boys Toys Week on the History Channel.

2
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:15,000
I'm in this body shop because it's here that cars, a boys toys favorite, are made to look their very best.

3
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:18,000
For the next few hours we've got more than just cars.

4
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:21,000
We're going to take an exciting look at private collections.

5
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:26,000
Everything from motorcycles to sports memorabilia to military vehicles.

6
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:32,740
We'll also show you Steven Seagal's guitars and 10,000 golf clubs that belong to Arnold Palmer.

7
00:00:33,200 --> 00:00:35,700
You don't want anything to stop you from missing it.

8
00:00:36,060 --> 00:00:39,200
It's coming up right now, only on the History Channel.

9
00:00:46,800 --> 00:00:52,460
They're classics, defined by power, speed, and brute force.

10
00:00:53,340 --> 00:00:55,840
This collection is not for the weak or tinnin'.

11
00:00:57,640 --> 00:01:01,220
These cars are just like their owner, all muscle.

12
00:01:03,640 --> 00:01:05,060
Why do I collect muscle cars?

13
00:01:06,740 --> 00:01:08,400
I think it fits the persona pretty well.

14
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:13,200
You know, I didn't intend it to be that way, but it's just the guy, the person that I am.

15
00:01:14,560 --> 00:01:17,860
Every car in his collection has an intimidating roar.

16
00:01:17,861 --> 00:01:26,160
With a few modern-day exceptions, he's based his collection on the cars of his youth.

17
00:01:26,640 --> 00:01:30,860
When I was a kid, most of my friends had older brothers.

18
00:01:31,540 --> 00:01:33,560
Most of those older brothers were into muscle cars.

19
00:01:34,520 --> 00:01:39,200
And I always looked up to them, you know, and always wanted to be like them because they had these cool cars.

20
00:01:39,201 --> 00:01:45,620
After success as an NFL football player and international stardom as a professional wrestler,

21
00:01:45,621 --> 00:01:49,620
Goldberg began the car collection of his dreams.

22
00:01:49,621 --> 00:01:53,620
I got to a position where I could fortunately purchase one.

23
00:01:53,621 --> 00:01:58,620
One led to two, and two led to five, and five led to ten, and I think tens led to twenty.

24
00:01:58,621 --> 00:02:00,183
You know, the sky's the limit.

25
00:02:00,184 --> 00:02:03,620
I personally don't believe I'll ever stop collecting these things.

26
00:02:03,621 --> 00:02:05,620
They're just so much fun.

27
00:02:06,620 --> 00:02:10,620
Muscle cars were first made in the 1960s.

28
00:02:11,280 --> 00:02:17,020
Often called supercars, they utilized high-performance factory engines built for race cars.

29
00:02:17,900 --> 00:02:24,120
The one that fits Goldberg the best is a customized clone of a 1970 Chrysler Challenger.

30
00:02:25,080 --> 00:02:28,840
I had built to my specifications exactly how I wanted it.

31
00:02:29,040 --> 00:02:32,800
Visually, performance-wise, it came out exactly how I wanted it.

32
00:02:32,940 --> 00:02:35,460
It couldn't be any more beautiful as far as I'm concerned.

33
00:02:35,461 --> 00:02:41,260
The body is original, but under the hood, it's far from standard.

34
00:02:42,060 --> 00:02:47,420
One thing that makes it special is that it's a 1968 date-coated true Hemi motor.

35
00:02:47,421 --> 00:02:49,500
I have a race radiator on it.

36
00:02:50,200 --> 00:02:52,500
The carburetor was made by Demon Carburetors.

37
00:02:52,820 --> 00:02:55,000
They put the nitrous system on it for me.

38
00:02:55,240 --> 00:02:56,760
This car is nothing but business.

39
00:02:58,760 --> 00:03:05,000
Named for the engine's hemispherical combustion chamber, Dodge's 5.7-liter Hemi V8

40
00:03:05,001 --> 00:03:07,500
produces 345 horsepower.

41
00:03:09,480 --> 00:03:11,700
Goldberg can add a quick burst of speed

42
00:03:11,701 --> 00:03:14,220
with his nitrous oxide injection system.

43
00:03:15,420 --> 00:03:19,020
It throws nitrous oxide right straight down through the carburetor,

44
00:03:19,180 --> 00:03:25,280
which gives you anywhere from a 50 to 300 to 400 horsepower boost.

45
00:03:25,920 --> 00:03:29,240
For Goldberg, each car is a reminder of his past.

46
00:03:29,660 --> 00:03:32,840
His Shelby GT500 evokes painful memories.

47
00:03:32,841 --> 00:03:35,400
There's a lot of emotion going into buying a car.

48
00:03:35,401 --> 00:03:38,460
Every day that I was sitting in my orthodontist chair as a kid,

49
00:03:38,620 --> 00:03:41,380
and he was cranking on my teeth and causing me so much pain,

50
00:03:41,840 --> 00:03:45,900
the only positive thing that I could derive from that experience

51
00:03:45,901 --> 00:03:51,640
was looking out the window at his 1967 GT500 that was British Racing Green.

52
00:03:51,900 --> 00:03:53,820
So guess what? Now I have one.

53
00:03:55,120 --> 00:03:57,920
Designed by champion race car driver Carroll Shelby,

54
00:03:57,921 --> 00:04:04,480
The Mustang GT is powered by a 500-horsepower 428 Supercobra jet engine.

55
00:04:05,680 --> 00:04:09,700
This is the one that I always envisioned buying when I started my collection.

56
00:04:10,120 --> 00:04:12,780
This was the one car that I wanted to obtain.

57
00:04:15,540 --> 00:04:18,320
For Goldberg, the thrill is in the fun.

58
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:21,000
Many of his cars are purchased at auction.

59
00:04:21,560 --> 00:04:25,200
It's an absolutely wonderful experience, and it's really hard not to get caught up in it.

60
00:04:25,201 --> 00:04:28,100
First year I went, I bought a car that I had no idea that I'd buy.

61
00:04:28,520 --> 00:04:34,900
Goldberg has enlisted Bob Johnson, a car broker, to search for the perfect cars to add to the collection.

62
00:04:35,700 --> 00:04:40,820
Anytime you are collecting muscle cars or collecting anything for that matter, there's always going to be forgeries.

63
00:04:40,980 --> 00:04:42,700
There's always going to be misrepresentations.

64
00:04:43,400 --> 00:04:46,420
You're always running that risk of being duped.

65
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:48,820
And fortunately, that's what Bob Johnson's for.

66
00:04:49,560 --> 00:04:54,040
Johnson helped with the purchase of a 1968 Camaro Supersport Yenco.

67
00:04:54,041 --> 00:04:56,680
for $106,000.

68
00:04:57,680 --> 00:05:00,780
It's a four-speed 427, totally original car,

69
00:05:01,320 --> 00:05:04,560
40,000 miles on it, clicked 40,000 when I got in the garage.

70
00:05:04,680 --> 00:05:06,180
I put 13 miles on it since.

71
00:05:06,440 --> 00:05:08,760
It's just one unbelievably fast car.

72
00:05:09,060 --> 00:05:10,660
It was basically made to drag race.

73
00:05:12,320 --> 00:05:17,480
There were only 32 1968 Chevy Yankos made with a 427 engine.

74
00:05:18,440 --> 00:05:21,140
But that's not the rarest vehicle in the collection.

75
00:05:21,141 --> 00:05:24,640
that distinction belongs to the lawman.

76
00:05:25,920 --> 00:05:30,760
The 1,000-horsepower 429 Superboss was commissioned by the military

77
00:05:30,761 --> 00:05:33,500
to teach soldiers how to handle a muscle car

78
00:05:33,501 --> 00:05:36,720
because of the growing number of racing casualties while on leave.

79
00:05:37,180 --> 00:05:38,740
Only two were ever made.

80
00:05:39,120 --> 00:05:40,420
One was crushed in transit.

81
00:05:40,720 --> 00:05:45,680
They were predominantly used in Air Force bases and aircraft carriers

82
00:05:45,681 --> 00:05:49,480
to make runs, you know, do quarter miles or do eighth miles.

83
00:05:49,481 --> 00:05:56,860
Purchased at auction for $118,000, the lawman is housed at the Muscle Car Museum in Tennessee.

84
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:01,000
It is the only car in the collection he's never driven.

85
00:06:02,060 --> 00:06:05,080
Honestly, the only reason why I haven't driven it is because I've never seen it.

86
00:06:05,820 --> 00:06:09,240
When I see it, I guarantee you I will get behind the wheel and I will drive that thing.

87
00:06:10,160 --> 00:06:14,420
The most expensive car in Goldberg's collection is a contemporary one.

88
00:06:14,680 --> 00:06:18,480
If you want a Ferrari, if you're not on the list, then you have to wait.

89
00:06:18,481 --> 00:06:24,080
you have to buy a used ferrari goldberg paid over 200 000 for the ferrari modena

90
00:06:24,880 --> 00:06:30,960
the car is powered by a 3.6 liter v8 engine for the top speed of 185 miles an hour

91
00:06:34,560 --> 00:06:38,400
listening to it smelling it driving it

92
00:06:41,200 --> 00:06:43,920
is almost as cool as just sitting here looking at it

93
00:06:43,921 --> 00:06:53,860
Like the Ferrari, Goldberg's 1959 Chevy Biscayne is painted his favorite color, black.

94
00:06:54,660 --> 00:06:56,400
That's a classic car, you know.

95
00:06:57,540 --> 00:06:59,500
All you gotta do is look at the fins in the back.

96
00:07:01,320 --> 00:07:02,640
This is my beach car.

97
00:07:03,260 --> 00:07:06,520
Seems as if it'd be pretty cool with my surfboard hanging out the back of it.

98
00:07:07,620 --> 00:07:12,560
Goldberg is in the process of relocating his collection from Atlanta, Georgia to Southern California.

99
00:07:13,920 --> 00:07:17,180
A 15-car garage is being built to house all the vehicles.

100
00:07:18,340 --> 00:07:19,860
Even though he collects for pleasure,

101
00:07:20,340 --> 00:07:23,360
there is a business side to owning these expensive cars.

102
00:07:24,040 --> 00:07:27,380
I find that these cars definitely hold their value

103
00:07:27,381 --> 00:07:29,480
in good and bad times in the economy.

104
00:07:30,020 --> 00:07:32,900
They're powerful and complement his muscular physique.

105
00:07:33,720 --> 00:07:38,120
This is one collection that seems to fit its owner's persona perfectly.

106
00:07:38,121 --> 00:07:45,220
As the sun sets on a world champions collection in the West,

107
00:07:45,900 --> 00:07:49,860
it soon rises on the larger-than-life collection of Steven Seagal.

108
00:07:51,040 --> 00:07:53,160
He's an international action superstar,

109
00:07:54,260 --> 00:07:57,140
known for his martial arts skills and signature ponytail.

110
00:07:58,240 --> 00:08:01,340
But when this big-screen hero isn't on the set of his latest movie,

111
00:08:01,920 --> 00:08:04,580
you'll most likely find him collecting the things he loves.

112
00:08:05,320 --> 00:08:07,740
I have some of the best swords in the world.

113
00:08:08,120 --> 00:08:11,960
and I have some of the best guitars in the world.

114
00:08:14,840 --> 00:08:17,920
I think he's happy with both the guitar and sword in his hand.

115
00:08:19,360 --> 00:08:20,640
One at a time, of course.

116
00:08:21,600 --> 00:08:25,820
His passion for swords began in the country where he sought enlightenment, Japan.

117
00:08:28,100 --> 00:08:33,040
I came here in the late 60s, early 70s, and stayed here until about 1985.

118
00:08:33,041 --> 00:08:39,040
I came here to study the martial arts, Aikido, Karate, Judo, Jutu II.

119
00:08:40,040 --> 00:08:43,040
Part of Seagal's martial arts training included the use of the sword.

120
00:08:44,040 --> 00:08:48,040
Once he began to master the weapon, he started his most beloved collection.

121
00:08:49,040 --> 00:08:56,040
I probably had 10 to 12 swords that were, within the history of Japan, amazingly important and significant.

122
00:08:56,041 --> 00:08:56,760
and significant.

123
00:09:02,100 --> 00:09:04,940
Samurai warriors emerged during the Heen period,

124
00:09:05,080 --> 00:09:07,300
from 794 to 1185.

125
00:09:08,560 --> 00:09:11,000
These warriors were sought after by feudal lords

126
00:09:11,001 --> 00:09:12,900
for protection from rival clans.

127
00:09:13,500 --> 00:09:15,500
Battles were constant and fierce.

128
00:09:16,840 --> 00:09:19,720
Many of the swords were modified to reduce the weight.

129
00:09:20,880 --> 00:09:24,360
Here you have a sword that's almost a thousand years old,

130
00:09:24,361 --> 00:09:27,700
and it feels, I can't even feel the weight in my hand.

131
00:09:28,320 --> 00:09:29,360
I feel like a feather.

132
00:09:30,200 --> 00:09:31,820
The balance is that good.

133
00:09:35,960 --> 00:09:39,020
Crafted by repeatedly heating and pounding steel,

134
00:09:39,640 --> 00:09:42,120
making a sword was more than just an act of labor.

135
00:09:42,860 --> 00:09:44,540
It was also a sacred ritual.

136
00:09:44,880 --> 00:09:46,600
When these sword masters made a sword,

137
00:09:46,700 --> 00:09:48,420
they didn't eat for sometimes weeks.

138
00:09:48,980 --> 00:09:51,940
They fasted, they prayed, they went inside the katana kaji

139
00:09:51,941 --> 00:09:57,860
and they made something that was supposed to be exemplary of their own spiritual quest.

140
00:09:58,860 --> 00:10:03,200
Upon completion, a blade would frequently be tested for sharpness and durability

141
00:10:03,201 --> 00:10:05,500
on the bodies of deceased criminals.

142
00:10:06,160 --> 00:10:08,320
The results were often documented on the handle.

143
00:10:09,240 --> 00:10:15,260
It'll say, this sword cut through five bodies as a test, and sometimes it's in gold.

144
00:10:17,460 --> 00:10:21,100
Seagal considers it an honor to own such ancient historic symbols.

145
00:10:21,101 --> 00:10:22,800
more than just weapons.

146
00:10:23,320 --> 00:10:25,760
He believes they are well-designed works of art.

147
00:10:26,300 --> 00:10:31,500
To us, it's like looking at a Picasso or a Rembrandt,

148
00:10:31,501 --> 00:10:32,480
but these are older.

149
00:10:34,460 --> 00:10:37,260
Collecting swords can be an expensive proposition.

150
00:10:38,420 --> 00:10:43,680
Swords can go upwards of a million just by virtue of what they are,

151
00:10:44,360 --> 00:10:46,300
not necessarily who they belong to.

152
00:10:46,620 --> 00:10:48,560
But swords aren't his only passion.

153
00:10:49,440 --> 00:10:52,900
He also has a collection of almost 300 guitars.

154
00:10:53,520 --> 00:10:55,620
Many of these instruments were hard to find.

155
00:10:56,200 --> 00:11:00,540
I collect guitars because I'm a serious player and I've been playing since I was a child.

156
00:11:01,360 --> 00:11:05,260
And I love guitar and I play all kinds of different music.

157
00:11:05,760 --> 00:11:09,460
Seagal uses his collection in the studio and on stage.

158
00:11:09,860 --> 00:11:11,760
Unlike other musicians and collectors,

159
00:11:12,080 --> 00:11:16,420
he is willing to risk some of his most prized guitars by taking them on the road.

160
00:11:16,421 --> 00:11:19,120
I travel with these, I just don't let them out of my sight.

161
00:11:20,320 --> 00:11:25,580
Not only are the guitars rare, they're made more valuable by the artists who played them.

162
00:11:26,440 --> 00:11:30,900
Celebrity guitars have value higher than non-celebrity guitars.

163
00:11:31,080 --> 00:11:38,520
That really doesn't relate to the value of the guitar as much as it relates to the aura of the celebrity who owned it or played it.

164
00:11:38,521 --> 00:11:42,280
The collection includes pieces once owned by Lenny Kravitz,

165
00:11:43,320 --> 00:11:44,280
Stevie Ray Vaughan,

166
00:11:44,880 --> 00:11:47,260
and Jimi Hendrix's Stratocaster.

167
00:11:47,680 --> 00:11:49,900
Jimi had thousands of strats,

168
00:11:50,160 --> 00:11:53,140
but he only had one jazz master since he became Jimi Hendrix,

169
00:11:53,220 --> 00:11:56,700
and this guitar is a real, real special feeling guitar.

170
00:11:58,020 --> 00:11:59,460
Seagal loves rock and roll,

171
00:11:59,860 --> 00:12:02,260
but the roots of his collection are in the blues.

172
00:12:02,261 --> 00:12:09,660
It's the people who made blues come to life in the United States of America and all over the world.

173
00:12:10,540 --> 00:12:16,580
He owns four guitars from B.B. King, which all hold his signature name of Lucille.

174
00:12:17,160 --> 00:12:18,500
I don't have one favorite.

175
00:12:18,820 --> 00:12:22,980
There are certain sort of spiritual guitars that have tremendous spiritual meaning to me,

176
00:12:23,040 --> 00:12:25,120
like the Tele that Albert Collins used a lot.

177
00:12:26,340 --> 00:12:29,860
Albert Collins earned the name the Master of the Telecaster.

178
00:12:29,861 --> 00:12:38,760
He himself put this kind of psychedelic sticker on here, and I think he was one of the greatest blues guitar players of our time.

179
00:12:39,320 --> 00:12:44,400
But Steven Seagal believes that the birth of the blues is represented elsewhere in his collection.

180
00:12:44,960 --> 00:12:50,940
I was born and raised in the blues, and so if you really play blues, I think you have to be able to play an acoustic also.

181
00:12:52,320 --> 00:12:55,880
Seagal owns many old acoustics, which are reminiscent of the blues.

182
00:12:55,881 --> 00:13:02,880
But some of the rarest and greatest acoustic guitars in Seagal's collection are manufactured by Martin guitars.

183
00:13:02,881 --> 00:13:07,880
I have a lot of old Martins, pre-war Martins.

184
00:13:07,881 --> 00:13:14,880
Seagal's pre-World War II D45 is considered one of the best sounding acoustic guitars in the world.

185
00:13:14,881 --> 00:13:20,880
It has the snowflake inlay on the neck, but it also has tuners that are factory tuners that are kind of gold.

186
00:13:20,881 --> 00:13:22,880
There's only a few of those in the world.

187
00:13:22,881 --> 00:13:29,360
You hear stories about D45s in particular, a guitar that sold new for $225,

188
00:13:30,040 --> 00:13:35,940
trading in the market for anywhere between $100,000 and $150,000, depending on the condition.

189
00:13:36,520 --> 00:13:39,960
C.F. Martin started his guitar company nearly two centuries ago.

190
00:13:40,620 --> 00:13:46,420
Since then, they have successfully produced many of the finest collectible guitars known to man.

191
00:13:47,100 --> 00:13:49,480
The craftsmanship is impeccable.

192
00:13:49,481 --> 00:13:57,100
Taking over two years to build, the 1 millionth Martin is the rarest and most valuable guitar they have ever crafted.

193
00:13:57,600 --> 00:14:02,600
It has 30 rubies, 40 diamonds, and is surrounded by 18-karat gold.

194
00:14:03,160 --> 00:14:08,080
It's such a magnificent piece of art, you know, you can't argue with it at all.

195
00:14:08,500 --> 00:14:10,060
I mean, this is a museum piece.

196
00:14:10,520 --> 00:14:12,540
They're going to be, you know, pretty rare.

197
00:14:12,541 --> 00:14:19,540
For Seagal, judging the collectability of guitars is a matter of personal taste.

198
00:14:19,541 --> 00:14:29,540
I collect the different kinds of guitars, not necessarily for how valuable they are, but by virtue of how they look and sound and play.

199
00:14:29,541 --> 00:14:37,540
Whether it's guitars or even swords, Steven Seagal's collections are a reflection of his favorite things in life.

200
00:14:37,541 --> 00:14:47,540
For me, it's a reverence and it's only natural to be able to collect those things and revere and admire them.

201
00:14:52,240 --> 00:14:58,140
Along with guitars and swords, Steven Seagal also collects Tibetan Buddhas.

202
00:15:00,340 --> 00:15:04,500
Modern Marvel's private collections will return next on The History Channel.

203
00:15:07,540 --> 00:15:16,780
Of all the collectors in the world, only a few can claim a collection of these.

204
00:15:19,780 --> 00:15:22,120
Airplanes, the classic kind.

205
00:15:28,640 --> 00:15:33,980
Collector and pilot Kermit Weeks has a remarkable collection of over 140 planes

206
00:15:33,981 --> 00:15:37,160
that span 100 years of aviation technology.

207
00:15:37,540 --> 00:15:42,240
And unlike most planes of this distinction that never leave the protection of an airplane hangar,

208
00:15:42,760 --> 00:15:45,780
all of Kermit's flying machines take to the sky.

209
00:15:49,840 --> 00:15:53,340
I don't collect anything that I don't intend to fly one day.

210
00:15:53,341 --> 00:15:58,080
So the airplanes in the collection either have flown, do fly, or will fly.

211
00:15:58,880 --> 00:16:03,020
Valued at over $40 million, with some of the planes considered priceless,

212
00:16:03,580 --> 00:16:07,020
Kermit Weeks has assembled the world's largest private collection.

213
00:16:07,540 --> 00:16:09,280
I think airplanes are kind of like people.

214
00:16:09,740 --> 00:16:13,120
They all have their own quirks, idiosyncrasies, qualities.

215
00:16:13,580 --> 00:16:14,680
You know, you have to love them all.

216
00:16:15,360 --> 00:16:18,300
Kermit was drawn to the world of aviation at age 13

217
00:16:18,301 --> 00:16:20,860
by the popular song Snoopy and the Red Baron.

218
00:16:21,380 --> 00:16:23,980
Little did he know it would change the course of his life.

219
00:16:24,500 --> 00:16:27,840
A spark lit inside me, and I just, at that point in my life,

220
00:16:27,841 --> 00:16:30,500
I became fascinated with aviation.

221
00:16:30,920 --> 00:16:34,380
By age 17, Kermit Weeks had built his first airplane

222
00:16:34,381 --> 00:16:39,780
and began performing aerial acrobatics in a dangerous sport called aerobatics.

223
00:16:40,680 --> 00:16:43,720
As I was competing on my high school gymnastic team,

224
00:16:44,240 --> 00:16:48,300
when I was used to flipping around in the gym, when I started to learn how to fly,

225
00:16:48,540 --> 00:16:51,440
I immediately gravitated towards flipping around in airplanes.

226
00:16:54,420 --> 00:16:58,380
Kermit's love for anything having to do with aviation took new flight

227
00:16:58,381 --> 00:17:03,080
when his grandfather shared his fortune from the oil business with the rest of the family.

228
00:17:03,081 --> 00:17:07,080
Kermit immediately started conducting airplanes.

229
00:17:07,081 --> 00:17:11,080
I began with one when I was about 25 years old.

230
00:17:11,081 --> 00:17:15,080
It led to another, another, and it just kind of snowballed

231
00:17:15,081 --> 00:17:17,080
and started going downhill from there.

232
00:17:17,081 --> 00:17:21,080
And that's how the collection process began.

233
00:17:21,081 --> 00:17:24,080
Many of his planes are authentic reproductions.

234
00:17:24,081 --> 00:17:28,080
Every effort is made to recreate the smallest detail.

235
00:17:28,081 --> 00:17:31,080
We have a number of airplanes in the collection

236
00:17:31,081 --> 00:17:33,120
in the collection from the early period

237
00:17:33,121 --> 00:17:35,160
that we built from scratch, but we always

238
00:17:35,161 --> 00:17:38,520
try and maintain a high degree of authenticity.

239
00:17:38,521 --> 00:17:41,320
The plane that first piqued his interest in aviation

240
00:17:41,321 --> 00:17:44,760
is now part of his collection, an exempt replica

241
00:17:44,761 --> 00:17:47,900
of the triplane flown by the German World War I flying

242
00:17:47,901 --> 00:17:50,800
ace Manfred von Richthofen.

243
00:17:50,801 --> 00:17:52,920
Known as the Red Baron, Richthofen

244
00:17:52,921 --> 00:17:56,080
led a German squadron often referred to as the Flying

245
00:17:56,081 --> 00:17:58,020
Circus.

246
00:17:58,021 --> 00:18:00,060
The Germans painted their airplanes up

247
00:18:00,061 --> 00:18:01,480
very gaudy colors.

248
00:18:01,900 --> 00:18:04,180
That's where they came up with the phrase the flying circus

249
00:18:04,181 --> 00:18:06,900
because they used to move around the front on trains

250
00:18:06,901 --> 00:18:09,880
and airplanes would show up in pieces

251
00:18:09,881 --> 00:18:12,700
kind of like a circus would when it arrived in town.

252
00:18:13,540 --> 00:18:16,120
Both World War I German aircraft in the collection

253
00:18:16,121 --> 00:18:17,600
utilize a unique engine.

254
00:18:18,340 --> 00:18:20,300
Normally the crankshaft goes around

255
00:18:20,301 --> 00:18:22,060
and is bolted to the propeller.

256
00:18:22,160 --> 00:18:25,920
On this particular engine, the crankshaft is fixed,

257
00:18:25,921 --> 00:18:28,120
the engine is bolted to the propeller,

258
00:18:28,121 --> 00:18:30,660
and the whole engine spins around with a propeller.

259
00:18:35,400 --> 00:18:39,880
The German World War I rotary engines do not have a throttling system.

260
00:18:40,820 --> 00:18:43,860
This makes for an airplane that can sound unsafe.

261
00:18:45,180 --> 00:18:48,240
The early ones were either full throttle on

262
00:18:48,241 --> 00:18:51,680
or you would actually shut the spark off of the spark plugs.

263
00:18:53,440 --> 00:18:55,760
It's a very unique sounding engine.

264
00:18:55,761 --> 00:18:58,180
and the pilot operates out with a button on the stick

265
00:18:58,181 --> 00:19:01,080
and a little five-position switch on the side of the cockpit.

266
00:19:04,900 --> 00:19:07,220
If pilots managed to survive their mission,

267
00:19:07,640 --> 00:19:10,160
they then had to face the challenge of landing the plane.

268
00:19:10,920 --> 00:19:14,420
No airplanes built prior to 1929 had brakes.

269
00:19:14,860 --> 00:19:17,760
I ended up going off the side of the runway,

270
00:19:17,880 --> 00:19:18,920
and I put it up on its nose.

271
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:19,840
I broke the propeller.

272
00:19:20,080 --> 00:19:21,320
This is a brand-new propeller,

273
00:19:21,500 --> 00:19:24,860
so hopefully the next time I'll have better success.

274
00:19:25,760 --> 00:19:32,860
With a list of aircraft that reads like an aviation who's who, Kermit's collection is

275
00:19:32,861 --> 00:19:36,720
a tribute to the innovation and imagination of the pioneers of flight.

276
00:19:36,721 --> 00:19:44,200
I actually have major focal points within the collection, like the largest private collection

277
00:19:44,201 --> 00:19:49,580
of vintage World War I airplanes, the largest private collection of World War II fighter

278
00:19:49,581 --> 00:19:56,640
airplanes, the largest bomber collection in the world, the largest collection of British

279
00:19:56,641 --> 00:19:58,520
airplanes in the world.

280
00:19:58,521 --> 00:20:03,080
Many of the aircraft serve as a reminder of historic and heroic events, like the tiny

281
00:20:03,081 --> 00:20:08,020
single-engine plane that Charles Lindbergh flew as the first person to make a solo non-stop

282
00:20:08,021 --> 00:20:10,180
flight across the Atlantic.

283
00:20:10,181 --> 00:20:11,480
The original Spirit of St.

284
00:20:11,481 --> 00:20:14,580
Louis, of course, is at the Smithsonian Institute, and rightly

285
00:20:14,581 --> 00:20:16,820
so, although I'd love to have it in my collection.

286
00:20:16,821 --> 00:20:18,740
But we have the next best thing.

287
00:20:18,741 --> 00:20:24,260
We have a very authentic reproduction of the airplane with all the original size fuel tanks.

288
00:20:24,600 --> 00:20:31,880
One of Lindbergh's greatest challenges was where to put the 400 gallons of fuel he would need to complete the 33 and a half hour nonstop flight.

289
00:20:32,600 --> 00:20:39,820
To keep the plane balanced as it emptied, the tank was placed at the plane's center of gravity, right in front of the pilot's seat, blocking his vision.

290
00:20:40,320 --> 00:20:44,760
So literally, Lindbergh is looking at 400 gallons of fuel when he's flying.

291
00:20:44,761 --> 00:20:50,540
They have two little windows on the side of the airplane, which he can look out of, and that's how you fly.

292
00:20:51,840 --> 00:20:59,620
Another famous replica is a plane that was destroyed while attempting to break the world speed record in 1931, the GBZ.

293
00:21:00,860 --> 00:21:02,460
It's very interesting to fly.

294
00:21:02,760 --> 00:21:05,980
It probably looks more like a cartoon character than any airplane that was built.

295
00:21:06,500 --> 00:21:10,540
And they took the biggest engine of the day and made the smallest airplane around it.

296
00:21:10,541 --> 00:21:14,660
Probably one of the most recognizable airplanes of the early racing period.

297
00:21:14,760 --> 00:21:22,860
The historic Ford Trimotor was used to transport passengers from New York to Los Angeles in the early 1930s.

298
00:21:24,120 --> 00:21:31,200
A lot of people got the first introduction safe flying when the airline's in a Ford Trimotor.

299
00:21:31,480 --> 00:21:35,700
A lot of the cables for the control systems are actually outside the airplane.

300
00:21:36,400 --> 00:21:42,020
Amelia Earhart greeted this exact plane in the promotional film Coast to Coast in 48 Hours.

301
00:21:42,021 --> 00:21:48,260
That same plane also starred with Harrison Ford at the controls in Indiana Jones and the Temple of Doom.

302
00:21:48,261 --> 00:21:51,860
The Ford Trimotor was one of the safest planes of its day.

303
00:21:51,861 --> 00:21:57,680
But other planes in the collection flew for the safety and security of the world.

304
00:21:57,681 --> 00:22:03,780
The airplane that I'm going to fly today is a very rare airplane.

305
00:22:03,781 --> 00:22:07,920
There's only four of these early original airplanes in the world.

306
00:22:07,921 --> 00:22:14,920
Kermit's P-51C is one of the rarest original planes in his collection, valued at over $2 million.

307
00:22:14,921 --> 00:22:21,920
These deadly, fast, high-flying, fuel-efficient planes were named Mustangs

308
00:22:21,921 --> 00:22:27,920
and were flown during World War II by the Tuskegee Airmen, the first black American fighter pilots.

309
00:22:27,921 --> 00:22:33,920
Their reputation for protecting bombers is legendary for never having lost a bomber to enemy aircraft.

310
00:22:33,921 --> 00:22:34,621
aircraft.

311
00:22:34,622 --> 00:22:40,000
This plane is painted in honor of pilot lead Buddy Archer, the highest scoring

312
00:22:40,001 --> 00:22:44,460
Tuskegee Airman. The symbols on the side of the plane represent his kills.

313
00:22:45,720 --> 00:22:48,970
It's so steeped in history. The performance is awesome.

314
00:22:48,971 --> 00:22:50,920
It was a state-of-the-art fighter

315
00:22:50,921 --> 00:22:52,577
at the end of World War II.

316
00:22:52,578 --> 00:22:56,260
I mean, this thing was built for a purpose, and it's a dream

317
00:22:56,261 --> 00:22:58,120
and a privilege to fly something like this.

318
00:22:58,121 --> 00:23:03,220
Another of Kermit's rare planes from World War II is a bomber with a notorious nickname,

319
00:23:03,221 --> 00:23:04,540
the Widowmaker.

320
00:23:05,160 --> 00:23:07,400
It's a Martin B-26 Marauder.

321
00:23:07,880 --> 00:23:11,520
They only probably have seven left on the planet.

322
00:23:11,900 --> 00:23:13,980
This is the only one that flies.

323
00:23:14,420 --> 00:23:17,540
They had a lot of training accidents in this airplane early on,

324
00:23:17,640 --> 00:23:20,940
but it actually had one of the best combat records

325
00:23:20,941 --> 00:23:24,100
of any of the medium bombers in the whole European theater

326
00:23:24,101 --> 00:23:25,900
for the American bomber.

327
00:23:26,300 --> 00:23:31,060
The plane that used to drop 5,000-pound bombs during World War II

328
00:23:31,061 --> 00:23:33,820
drops a very different type of ammo today.

329
00:23:34,120 --> 00:23:37,920
We take it down to an air show in Fort Lauderdale once a year.

330
00:23:38,100 --> 00:23:41,620
We actually drop watermelons out of it today, so still bombing.

331
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:46,980
Playing around on these planes can cost a pretty penny.

332
00:23:47,880 --> 00:23:49,980
With twin 2,000-horsepower engines,

333
00:23:50,420 --> 00:23:54,820
the aircraft consumes 160 gallons of gasoline per hour.

334
00:23:55,500 --> 00:23:59,720
It's expensive, but the reality is the airplanes are so expensive.

335
00:23:59,721 --> 00:24:04,280
So if you have to ask how much it costs in fuel, you're not in the market to buy one.

336
00:24:05,200 --> 00:24:11,020
The largest airplane in Kermit's collection is actually a flying boat called the Short Sunderbite.

337
00:24:11,500 --> 00:24:16,000
It's the last four-engine passenger flying boat that still flies on the planet.

338
00:24:16,001 --> 00:24:21,100
We brought it back across the Atlantic Ocean from England ten years ago.

339
00:24:21,460 --> 00:24:27,320
It was the last crossing of a flying boat, a four-engine flying boat, over a major ocean.

340
00:24:27,321 --> 00:24:30,680
Kermit was able to pilot the flying boat back to Florida,

341
00:24:30,681 --> 00:24:33,220
but other planes still await the journey.

342
00:24:33,221 --> 00:24:36,320
Actually, a lot of the collection is not here on site.

343
00:24:36,321 --> 00:24:39,020
I've got airplanes stashed around the world.

344
00:24:39,021 --> 00:24:42,520
Ultimately, we hope to house everything here in Central Florida.

345
00:24:42,521 --> 00:24:46,720
As amazing as the collection is, it was almost decimated

346
00:24:46,721 --> 00:24:50,720
when Hurricane Andrew struck Miami in 1992.

347
00:24:50,721 --> 00:24:54,220
The hangar was completely destroyed,

348
00:24:54,221 --> 00:24:57,380
completely fell in on all my airplanes.

349
00:24:57,880 --> 00:25:01,260
A number of airplanes outside were blown over a mile away.

350
00:25:01,760 --> 00:25:03,580
There was millions of dollars in damage,

351
00:25:04,020 --> 00:25:07,000
and most of the collection was not covered by insurance.

352
00:25:07,540 --> 00:25:12,260
There was about 30 minutes of tears and utter just disbelief, really,

353
00:25:12,440 --> 00:25:15,620
and then there was so much work to do.

354
00:25:15,621 --> 00:25:17,640
Most of the collection is now repaired.

355
00:25:18,420 --> 00:25:21,880
It's housed and displayed at Fantasy of Flight in Central Florida.

356
00:25:21,881 --> 00:25:27,880
The collection really transcends value and ownership to me.

357
00:25:27,881 --> 00:25:31,880
And I really hope I can use them in a way that can touch people in a profound way.

358
00:25:31,881 --> 00:25:39,880
Kermit's collection speaks for heroes in our history who never gave up, pushed their personal limits, and soared to their ultimate dreams.

359
00:25:42,880 --> 00:25:48,880
The B-29 Superfortress is the biggest and most fuel-consuming plane in Kermit Weeks' collection.

360
00:25:48,881 --> 00:25:53,120
collection. It consumes roughly 350 gallons per hour.

361
00:25:54,280 --> 00:25:58,580
Modern Marvel's private collections will return next on the History Channel.

362
00:26:10,080 --> 00:26:16,680
This collection has raced, jumped and crashed in movies and on racetracks around the world.

363
00:26:16,681 --> 00:26:20,160
Motorcycles. More than 200 of them.

364
00:26:22,800 --> 00:26:25,880
All owned by a daredevil turned stuntman.

365
00:26:26,420 --> 00:26:31,260
There's no question that motorcycles started my career and are still a very big part of it.

366
00:26:32,120 --> 00:26:33,800
His profession is his collection.

367
00:26:34,480 --> 00:26:37,780
And for Gary Davis, motorcycles are a good life.

368
00:26:38,320 --> 00:26:42,320
People say that I have a big collection of motorcycles or they say this is a motorcycle museum.

369
00:26:42,900 --> 00:26:45,540
Truth of the matter is, this is just my toy box.

370
00:26:46,680 --> 00:26:55,440
Davis' giant toy box is a 9,000-square-foot shop next to his Northern California home.

371
00:26:55,441 --> 00:26:59,740
The collection is relatively new, but he's been playing with motorcycles since he was

372
00:26:59,741 --> 00:27:00,740
14.

373
00:27:00,741 --> 00:27:06,180
When I was a kid in high school, I had a 160 Honda, but if you were cool, you had a

374
00:27:06,181 --> 00:27:08,740
Gold Star, and I couldn't afford a Gold Star.

375
00:27:08,741 --> 00:27:14,300
The BSA Gold Star DBD 34 was the most advanced motorcycle engine of its time.

376
00:27:14,301 --> 00:27:18,460
dominated racing during the 60s and was one of the last gold stars ever produced.

377
00:27:18,940 --> 00:27:21,420
I have a few of them, and I purchased this one recently.

378
00:27:21,860 --> 00:27:23,860
It's kind of rough looking, as you can see.

379
00:27:24,560 --> 00:27:29,760
All my gold stars are DBD 34, which is the sought-after motors.

380
00:27:30,820 --> 00:27:35,120
Even though while growing up he didn't have his dream bike, Davis had riding talent.

381
00:27:35,300 --> 00:27:40,260
You have to be subtle about the movements you make on a motorcycle and stuff,

382
00:27:40,660 --> 00:27:43,360
and for some reason it came fairly natural to me.

383
00:27:43,361 --> 00:27:46,500
All through high school, riding was just for fun.

384
00:27:47,300 --> 00:27:49,700
When he started racing, things turned serious.

385
00:27:50,160 --> 00:27:51,540
I got second in my first race.

386
00:27:52,060 --> 00:27:53,400
He went on to be state champion.

387
00:27:54,140 --> 00:27:55,260
Soon, he was pro.

388
00:27:56,040 --> 00:27:59,400
But racing wasn't the only way to make a living in 1969.

389
00:28:03,120 --> 00:28:07,400
Top daredevil, Evel Knievel, was getting rich jumping over cars.

390
00:28:08,360 --> 00:28:09,920
Gary felt he could do even better.

391
00:28:10,360 --> 00:28:12,780
But it meant taking riding to the extreme.

392
00:28:12,781 --> 00:28:18,740
In 1971, I started jumping motorcycles over cars for a living, a la Evel Knievel.

393
00:28:20,460 --> 00:28:23,360
My partner, Rex Blackwell, and myself used a cross in the air.

394
00:28:24,040 --> 00:28:32,340
And on our third jump, we went for the world record and jumped 21 cars, beating Evel's 19-car record at Ontario Motor Speedway.

395
00:28:34,560 --> 00:28:41,260
The 1971 350 Honda Twin Davis Road to break the world record is still part of his collection.

396
00:28:41,261 --> 00:28:46,360
For a short time, Davis and Knievel continuously tried to out-compete each other.

397
00:28:46,900 --> 00:28:53,020
But when Hollywood came calling, Davis jumped at the chance to perform stunts in a movie about the legendary daredevil.

398
00:28:53,380 --> 00:28:59,320
They needed a stuntman to come in and jump 120 feet while they blew both ramps up.

399
00:29:00,000 --> 00:29:07,300
And it sounded like an exciting endeavor, so I therefore joined the picture business.

400
00:29:07,301 --> 00:29:12,300
Gary thought he had done just about everything there was to do on a motorcycle.

401
00:29:12,301 --> 00:29:16,300
But the movie business forced him to do something he had always avoided.

402
00:29:16,301 --> 00:29:19,300
doubling Knievel, I had to throw the bike away at very high speeds.

403
00:29:19,301 --> 00:29:22,300
But I've never ever really been hurt on a bike at all.

404
00:29:22,301 --> 00:29:24,300
I've never broken any bones on a motorcycle.

405
00:29:24,301 --> 00:29:29,300
To date, he has worked on over 300 movies and TV shows.

406
00:29:29,301 --> 00:29:34,300
Now a stunt coordinator and director, he's been able to employ his collection.

407
00:29:34,301 --> 00:29:44,100
Most of my newer bikes were used in lots of films, but up on the top shelf up there, there's the bike I used as Captain America, and we did two different pilots.

408
00:29:44,580 --> 00:29:50,440
This 1976 make-up appeared with Gary in one of the very first movies about motocross.

409
00:29:50,840 --> 00:29:55,700
We built five bikes that were called Sidewinder 1s for a movie called Sidewinder 1, and I only kept the one.

410
00:29:56,940 --> 00:29:59,620
Davis has also collected Evel Knievel memorabilia.

411
00:30:00,440 --> 00:30:03,040
No longer rivals. Today, they are friends.

412
00:30:03,041 --> 00:30:12,680
So I have, you know, his toys and his lunch boxes and posters and costumes and all kinds of stuff that kids would have had at that time.

413
00:30:13,280 --> 00:30:18,640
Even though he loves the movie business, Gary really needs his downtime between pictures.

414
00:30:19,080 --> 00:30:25,240
If I didn't have my motorcycles to come down here and work on every day, I'd have to be going to a shrink a couple of times a week.

415
00:30:26,160 --> 00:30:28,600
There is no shortage of bikes to work on.

416
00:30:29,420 --> 00:30:32,540
Many, like this Vincent Lightning, are works in progress.

417
00:30:33,040 --> 00:30:35,860
They're a fairly sought-after bike. They're highly respected.

418
00:30:36,340 --> 00:30:39,360
They were ahead of their time in almost everything they did.

419
00:30:39,640 --> 00:30:44,300
Their suspension, if you look at the suspension, is done like when Yamaha was so proud of themselves

420
00:30:44,301 --> 00:30:49,300
and they called themselves a monoshock, they built this triangular structured swing arm

421
00:30:49,301 --> 00:30:51,540
with a single shock that ran up underneath the tank.

422
00:30:52,140 --> 00:30:57,080
Well, here's a bike from the early 50s that has the same exact suspension.

423
00:30:57,860 --> 00:31:00,840
When completed, the Vincent will be a legal street bike.

424
00:31:01,540 --> 00:31:04,120
Many of the motorcycles take a long time to finish.

425
00:31:04,800 --> 00:31:07,800
Gary pieces them together, one part at a time.

426
00:31:08,560 --> 00:31:11,440
If something is missing, he'll place an order for the part.

427
00:31:12,060 --> 00:31:15,100
But by the time it arrives, he's off working on the next.

428
00:31:15,640 --> 00:31:17,860
Most of the bikes I buy are in pretty rough shape, actually,

429
00:31:18,000 --> 00:31:21,180
because it's no fun for me to buy a restored motorcycle.

430
00:31:22,840 --> 00:31:26,160
That's somebody else's labor of love.

431
00:31:26,161 --> 00:31:33,160
When restoring a motorcycle, Gary is willing to make alterations that purists wouldn't like.

432
00:31:33,161 --> 00:31:40,160
On something like this Vincent, 90% of this bike is accurate to the era.

433
00:31:40,161 --> 00:31:44,160
However, I have aluminum wheels and stainless steel spokes, aluminum hubs,

434
00:31:44,161 --> 00:31:50,160
and things that it didn't necessarily have that I personally want in slightly later model carburetors.

435
00:31:50,161 --> 00:31:55,160
A true collector would diminish the points on my bike.

436
00:31:55,161 --> 00:31:59,200
Another valuable bike in Gary's collection is the 1965 Triumph.

437
00:31:59,840 --> 00:32:02,860
This is the most sought after of the stock Triumph frames.

438
00:32:03,060 --> 00:32:07,420
This is the frame that when Trackmaster first started, they designed all their geometry off of.

439
00:32:07,920 --> 00:32:11,760
Triumph prior to this year and after this year, always had the forks raked out.

440
00:32:12,380 --> 00:32:15,820
Bike after bike, each one slightly different from the next.

441
00:32:16,300 --> 00:32:18,920
They almost all have one thing in common.

442
00:32:19,560 --> 00:32:24,120
In both motocross and in flat track, I'm 89.

443
00:32:24,121 --> 00:32:25,918
That's my current racing number.

444
00:32:25,919 --> 00:32:29,120
That's the number I rode when I was a professional racer.

445
00:32:29,121 --> 00:32:34,120
And then in road race, it was already taken, so I'm 890.

446
00:32:34,121 --> 00:32:39,120
And then in trials, I just run a number two on the front,

447
00:32:39,121 --> 00:32:42,120
because that's the line that I run in the trials events.

448
00:32:42,121 --> 00:32:46,120
If he's not working on his collection, Gary can be found on the track,

449
00:32:46,121 --> 00:32:49,120
testing his motorcycles prior to a race.

450
00:32:49,121 --> 00:32:52,120
We'll go out and make a few laps and see if she still runs anyway.

451
00:32:52,121 --> 00:32:55,320
Anyway, this is my little 350 Honda single,

452
00:32:55,321 --> 00:32:58,500
and it's in a Trackmaster frame, Trackmaster forks.

453
00:32:58,501 --> 00:33:02,520
And all my bikes have the Kickstarters removed

454
00:33:02,521 --> 00:33:03,960
for flat trackers and my road racers,

455
00:33:03,961 --> 00:33:05,740
so I use this to start them,

456
00:33:05,741 --> 00:33:07,000
so I don't have to push them all the time,

457
00:33:07,001 --> 00:33:09,780
because I'm getting too old to run behind them, you know?

458
00:33:09,781 --> 00:33:11,780
Let's see if I can even get her to run.

459
00:33:22,120 --> 00:33:25,120
I was being very cautious, you know.

460
00:33:25,121 --> 00:33:28,120
When the throttle started sticking and getting me a little close to the edges of the throttle on,

461
00:33:28,121 --> 00:33:30,120
I decided it was time to quit.

462
00:33:32,120 --> 00:33:39,120
The oldest and rarest motorcycles Gary races are part of a class named after a prehistoric creature.

463
00:33:39,121 --> 00:33:44,120
The dinosaur class is a pre-51 or earlier motorcycle.

464
00:33:44,121 --> 00:33:50,120
And it's a 500cc limit with the exception of Harley-Davidson and Indians are allowed to run 750cc.

465
00:33:50,121 --> 00:33:52,360
That's the 1951 Triumph 500.

466
00:33:52,361 --> 00:33:55,680
It's a rigid frame and it runs the Dinosaur class.

467
00:33:55,681 --> 00:33:59,800
Sometimes he will combine bikes for better performance.

468
00:33:59,801 --> 00:34:05,820
This motorcycle is what they call a Tribsudz because it's a Triumph 650 motorcycle engine

469
00:34:05,821 --> 00:34:08,240
in a BSA A10 chassis.

470
00:34:08,241 --> 00:34:11,520
It handles like a gold star except it has the Triumph twin motor in it.

471
00:34:11,521 --> 00:34:16,180
They're two of my favorite motocrossers actually, although they're both pretty heavy for it

472
00:34:16,181 --> 00:34:19,960
to be thrown around at a constant pace.

473
00:34:19,961 --> 00:34:23,820
All the bikes in the collection are serious riding machines, except for one.

474
00:34:24,540 --> 00:34:27,280
I have a little road racer that's called a pocket bike.

475
00:34:27,420 --> 00:34:30,840
It's about two foot long and 16 inches high.

476
00:34:32,460 --> 00:34:35,580
It's almost impossible to ride from a guy my size.

477
00:34:36,320 --> 00:34:41,360
Davis also has an extremely rare collectible, which he believes is a museum piece.

478
00:34:41,880 --> 00:34:44,940
The Yamaha YDS-1R is one of my prized possessions.

479
00:34:44,941 --> 00:34:47,160
It's one of only three known to exist in America.

480
00:34:47,161 --> 00:34:51,480
It is the first production road racer Yamaha built.

481
00:34:52,480 --> 00:34:57,200
It's the highest classed motorcycle I have in my collection.

482
00:34:59,240 --> 00:35:04,140
A sentimental favorite is a 1918 Indian known as a flat tracker.

483
00:35:05,100 --> 00:35:10,700
Davis likes it so much, he separated it from the rest of his collection and keeps it in his house.

484
00:35:11,120 --> 00:35:12,980
I love the era as much as I do the bike.

485
00:35:12,981 --> 00:35:19,040
I love the way that racing was treated in those days, that it was a high-class event.

486
00:35:19,780 --> 00:35:27,440
In the early days in board track racing, the bikes were nothing more than a bicycle with a big V-twin engine in it.

487
00:35:31,120 --> 00:35:38,240
Built by a legend in motorcycles, the 1956 BSA Gold Star holds the greatest sentimental value to Davis.

488
00:35:38,241 --> 00:35:40,163
This bike was built by Ray Hensley.

489
00:35:40,164 --> 00:35:43,680
He was the founder of both Sonic Weld and of Trackmaster Frames.

490
00:35:44,140 --> 00:35:48,060
It was the last motorcycle that he assembled before his death.

491
00:35:48,900 --> 00:35:52,600
And I miss him a lot, and this bike will never be away from me.

492
00:35:58,060 --> 00:36:03,480
Davis does have a few modern bikes in his collection, but that doesn't mean they sit well with him.

493
00:36:03,481 --> 00:36:08,680
This is an enduro model that I just use for playing around on the ranch.

494
00:36:08,681 --> 00:36:11,980
This is a modern bike, so it feels alien to me already.

495
00:36:11,981 --> 00:36:14,880
I mean, it's a nice cush bike, but I'm not used to that.

496
00:36:14,881 --> 00:36:19,380
As long as Davis is around his toys, he feels like a big kid.

497
00:36:19,381 --> 00:36:21,080
I don't want to ever grow up.

498
00:36:21,081 --> 00:36:27,080
With a collection this size, he'll be playing with motorcycles for the rest of his life.

499
00:36:27,081 --> 00:36:37,600
Gary Davis has broken his back and shattered his ankle while performing stunts for TV and movies.

500
00:36:38,080 --> 00:36:40,860
But he's never been injured while riding a motorcycle.

501
00:36:42,720 --> 00:36:46,840
Modern Marvel's private collections will return next on the History Channel.

502
00:36:52,120 --> 00:36:53,940
They're made to go anywhere.

503
00:36:55,660 --> 00:36:56,900
Across land.

504
00:36:57,080 --> 00:36:59,340
over water

505
00:36:59,341 --> 00:37:02,460
and through just about anything.

506
00:37:03,960 --> 00:37:05,280
Get out of the way.

507
00:37:06,420 --> 00:37:08,540
This collection is unstoppable.

508
00:37:09,340 --> 00:37:11,940
Some people say it's strange, but it's definitely boys and their toys.

509
00:37:12,880 --> 00:37:15,960
It's every boy's fantasy to play with toys like these.

510
00:37:16,440 --> 00:37:19,460
For Charles Burnett III, it's a dream come true.

511
00:37:20,020 --> 00:37:22,140
He owns a personal armored fleet,

512
00:37:22,141 --> 00:37:25,640
a virtual army of more than 30 military vehicles.

513
00:37:27,080 --> 00:37:33,080
They use treads, tracks, and propellers to go almost anywhere.

514
00:37:33,081 --> 00:37:41,080
In my collection, I'm particularly interested in vehicles that are designed to get through the most challenging and tough environments,

515
00:37:41,081 --> 00:37:45,080
whether it be ice or snow or swamps or water.

516
00:37:45,081 --> 00:37:56,080
With a total weight of more than 200 tons, the collection of tanks and multipurpose military craft are all housed near Burnett's spectacular home in southern England.

517
00:37:56,081 --> 00:38:00,080
Burnett's own backyard provides the perfect battlefield.

518
00:38:00,081 --> 00:38:04,080
This is why it's some of the best testing grounds in the world here,

519
00:38:04,081 --> 00:38:08,080
because you've got mud, you've got rock, you've got water.

520
00:38:08,081 --> 00:38:12,080
So, if you can get through this, you can get through just about anything.

521
00:38:12,081 --> 00:38:18,080
The terrain is ideal, but that isn't the only reason the Texan keeps his collection at his second home.

522
00:38:18,081 --> 00:38:24,080
One of the great things about England is that driving track vehicles is not specifically prohibited by law,

523
00:38:24,081 --> 00:38:26,080
as long as you have the correct license, isn't it?

524
00:38:26,081 --> 00:38:30,080
It's not uncommon to see Burnett in one of his massive machines

525
00:38:30,081 --> 00:38:32,080
as he takes a joyride through town.

526
00:38:32,081 --> 00:38:35,080
But not everyone in the neighborhood shares his enthusiasm.

527
00:38:35,081 --> 00:38:37,080
It's a pretty conservative area around here,

528
00:38:37,081 --> 00:38:39,080
so I bet a lot of people think he's pretty strange.

529
00:38:39,081 --> 00:38:43,080
But most of the people of my generation take him pretty much

530
00:38:43,081 --> 00:38:47,080
just as a bit of an odd bod who, uh, he's a bit wacky

531
00:38:47,081 --> 00:38:49,080
and he likes to have a good time.

532
00:38:49,081 --> 00:38:51,080
I'm considered a bit on the eccentric side,

533
00:38:51,081 --> 00:38:55,160
eccentric side but as long as I'm not rolling in over anybody's front garden

534
00:38:55,161 --> 00:38:59,620
they're not gonna be too upset about it. Burnett uses his war machines to go up,

535
00:38:59,621 --> 00:39:08,240
over, through, and into just about anything. For fun he stages battles.

536
00:39:08,241 --> 00:39:14,580
Each vehicle in his collection excels in specific combat situations. If I was in a

537
00:39:14,581 --> 00:39:20,000
swamp condition and needed to travel fast certainly the American otter is the

538
00:39:20,001 --> 00:39:26,494
one I'd pick. The M76 Otter was produced from 1942 until 1953. U.S.

539
00:39:26,495 --> 00:39:28,820
forces utilized over 200

540
00:39:28,821 --> 00:39:30,675
otters during the Korean War.

541
00:39:30,676 --> 00:39:34,960
The vehicle carried up to four tons of cargo, but its thin aluminum

542
00:39:34,961 --> 00:39:37,014
plating made it vulnerable to attack.

543
00:39:37,015 --> 00:39:40,400
If you slam the rakes on hard enough, you can actually tip it

544
00:39:40,401 --> 00:39:44,331
right up onto its nose, and then it'll flop back down again.

545
00:39:44,332 --> 00:39:46,820
On land, rubber tires and tracks power

546
00:39:46,821 --> 00:39:49,480
the otter to speeds up to 30 miles per hour.

547
00:39:49,481 --> 00:39:52,260
In water, it can go just about anywhere except

548
00:39:52,261 --> 00:39:53,258
for the open sea.

549
00:39:53,259 --> 00:39:57,780
That's for another of Burnett's military vehicles, the English-made Stalwart.

550
00:39:58,220 --> 00:40:03,743
It has four-wheel steering, six-wheel drive. It's a great cross-country vehicle.

551
00:40:03,744 --> 00:40:04,640
It swims with

552
00:40:04,641 --> 00:40:09,260
the help of twin jet drives, so you can get up to six, seven miles an hour in the water,

553
00:40:09,760 --> 00:40:12,172
and it carries about 10 tons in the back.

554
00:40:12,173 --> 00:40:14,760
The Stalwart followed tanks into battle with

555
00:40:14,761 --> 00:40:16,520
supplies of ammunition and fuel.

556
00:40:17,160 --> 00:40:19,960
From the late 1960s to the early 1990s,

557
00:40:20,240 --> 00:40:22,100
the British Army utilized the stalwart.

558
00:40:22,620 --> 00:40:24,900
It appeared in British campaigns in Belize,

559
00:40:25,320 --> 00:40:26,900
the Falklands, and Cyprus.

560
00:40:27,700 --> 00:40:28,740
So it may look like a submarine,

561
00:40:28,900 --> 00:40:31,160
but actually, in practice, you don't close the hatches

562
00:40:31,161 --> 00:40:32,660
because in case it does sink,

563
00:40:32,800 --> 00:40:33,900
you want to be able to get out again.

564
00:40:34,920 --> 00:40:36,660
Not far from his backyard,

565
00:40:37,060 --> 00:40:40,200
Burnett tests the stalwart and other large amphibious craft

566
00:40:40,201 --> 00:40:42,740
in deep water that leads into the English Channel.

567
00:40:42,741 --> 00:40:47,740
It's rare for his vehicles to sink, but there are other risks when entering the water.

568
00:40:47,741 --> 00:40:50,740
The first thing to check is to make sure that you can get out again.

569
00:40:50,741 --> 00:40:55,740
So we want to make sure that wherever we have to come back to is dry and firm enough to get us out.

570
00:40:55,741 --> 00:40:58,740
We want to make sure there's not a big tidal run going.

571
00:40:58,741 --> 00:41:00,740
Seven, eight knot tides are not uncommon out here.

572
00:41:00,741 --> 00:41:06,740
So with a vehicle that only goes five miles an hour, you can readily understand how you can end up in the English Channel if you're not careful.

573
00:41:06,741 --> 00:41:08,740
You've got no water drive, is that right? Come back.

574
00:41:08,741 --> 00:41:09,740
That's correct.

575
00:41:09,741 --> 00:41:11,740
Okay. We'll be on our way over.

576
00:41:11,741 --> 00:41:16,740
This is really treacherous mud, some of the worst in the world.

577
00:41:16,741 --> 00:41:20,740
It gets high tide under it, and it gets very loose and soft,

578
00:41:20,741 --> 00:41:24,740
so you can't use the tracks or the wheels to get you out.

579
00:41:24,741 --> 00:41:28,740
So you have to rely on your drives or somebody else that's got more traction than you do.

580
00:41:28,741 --> 00:41:30,740
So at the moment, we probably have more traction.

581
00:41:30,741 --> 00:41:34,740
Being a track vehicle, we're going to go and give them a little bit of a help there,

582
00:41:34,741 --> 00:41:39,740
drag them out of the mud and keep from having to wait for the rising tide to bring us in.

583
00:41:39,741 --> 00:41:46,320
In difficult situations like these, Burnett deploys a massive machine called the AquaTrack.

584
00:41:51,000 --> 00:41:54,160
It's the only vehicle in the world with seven stations.

585
00:41:54,940 --> 00:41:58,580
That means it has actually seven wheels in contact with the ground.

586
00:41:59,920 --> 00:42:06,420
The later prototypes were actually made too wide to legally go on the road.

587
00:42:06,421 --> 00:42:12,400
This is legal by one millimeter on each side, as long as the yellow beacon is turning up the top.

588
00:42:13,780 --> 00:42:19,180
One of the rarest vehicles in Burnett's collection, the Alcatrack was never put into production.

589
00:42:19,840 --> 00:42:21,440
Only three were ever made.

590
00:42:22,700 --> 00:42:27,380
Diesel-powered, the Alcatrack has a top land speed of over 40 miles per hour.

591
00:42:28,580 --> 00:42:32,120
Most of the vehicles in Burnett's collection perform best on land,

592
00:42:32,121 --> 00:42:36,680
And the Snowcat was made to handle some of Mother Nature's worst conditions.

593
00:42:37,300 --> 00:42:41,920
The Snowcat was probably more designed to cover snow and ice than it was to be truly amphibious.

594
00:42:42,460 --> 00:42:46,320
But having said that, it will cross still lakes with relatively ease.

595
00:42:47,120 --> 00:42:53,480
Utilized by the British Army in the Falklands in both Gulf Wars, the Snowcat moves across land at 40 miles an hour.

596
00:42:54,380 --> 00:42:57,560
Automatic transmission makes it easy for even a novice to drive.

597
00:42:58,620 --> 00:43:01,760
It's not armored, but it's very light, mobile, and agile.

598
00:43:02,120 --> 00:43:04,460
and gets up and down most banks.

599
00:43:06,700 --> 00:43:10,040
A surprising number of dry land military vehicles

600
00:43:10,041 --> 00:43:13,540
are capable of amphibious activity, even this Jeep.

601
00:43:14,840 --> 00:43:18,920
This is a 50s-era Jeep that we got from the Dutch Army.

602
00:43:19,520 --> 00:43:20,900
It's quite a capable vehicle.

603
00:43:21,460 --> 00:43:24,180
By the 50s, they developed a fording capacity.

604
00:43:24,560 --> 00:43:26,380
In fact, there's a lever on the dash that you can pull.

605
00:43:27,500 --> 00:43:30,820
It pressurizes the differential in the engine

606
00:43:30,821 --> 00:43:31,800
to keep the water out.

607
00:43:32,120 --> 00:43:34,800
And with a snorkel, you can actually completely submerge the vehicle.

608
00:43:35,180 --> 00:43:41,240
With a weight of 17 tons, the Abbott self-propelled gun has a firing range of over 9 miles.

609
00:43:42,800 --> 00:43:47,300
Used by armies around the world, it can go just about anywhere in an emergency.

610
00:43:48,120 --> 00:43:49,240
It will actually float.

611
00:43:50,240 --> 00:43:52,860
There are poles that go around in these holders at the front.

612
00:43:53,640 --> 00:43:55,560
And there's a screen that goes on it.

613
00:43:56,440 --> 00:44:00,000
And with that, it has enough buoyancy that it will actually stay above the water, if you're lucky.

614
00:44:00,960 --> 00:44:06,520
Another tank, the Spartan, is effective on soft ground, despite a weight of eight tons.

615
00:44:07,220 --> 00:44:09,840
It is also an armored vehicle, although not heavily armored.

616
00:44:10,320 --> 00:44:16,040
Mainly used for reconnaissance and quick thrust behind enemy line,

617
00:44:16,120 --> 00:44:19,760
but not something that you want to get stuck in as the rocket-propelled grenades are going off.

618
00:44:20,700 --> 00:44:26,140
The Spartan can travel up to 60 miles per hour, thanks to a souped-up Jaguar engine.

619
00:44:26,141 --> 00:44:31,140
But that isn't the only vehicle in the collection associated with a high-performance automobile.

620
00:44:31,141 --> 00:44:35,140
The Ferret is powered by a Rolls-Royce engine.

621
00:44:35,141 --> 00:44:40,140
It's a fun vehicle to drive. It's light. It's nimble.

622
00:44:40,141 --> 00:44:45,140
You can take a crew of up to three and also able to drive it on the roads here,

623
00:44:45,141 --> 00:44:48,140
license it, as you can see, for road use quite happily.

624
00:44:48,141 --> 00:44:53,140
And as long as you take care of not getting too many fender benders.

625
00:44:53,141 --> 00:44:58,740
For Charles Burnett, the vehicles are made to be driven, and he wouldn't have it any other way.

626
00:44:59,200 --> 00:45:01,760
But the machines aren't always the easiest to operate.

627
00:45:02,560 --> 00:45:08,260
This is something from the Eastern Bloc, the staple of their reconnaissance scout cars with these thugs.

628
00:45:09,020 --> 00:45:13,580
They also came armed, and they had an interesting characteristic.

629
00:45:13,780 --> 00:45:19,220
They have pneumatic wheels that will drop down to try and get yourself out of a hole that you might have got into.

630
00:45:19,221 --> 00:45:27,060
First built in 1968 for the Hungarian military, many fogs are still used by Eastern European countries.

631
00:45:28,280 --> 00:45:30,640
Burnett purchased an extra just for spare parts.

632
00:45:31,700 --> 00:45:36,540
We can't understand a lot of the directions because they're all in Russian or Polish on the plates inside.

633
00:45:38,440 --> 00:45:40,980
Having your own armada can be a lot of fun.

634
00:45:41,520 --> 00:45:44,420
But at the end of the day, there's still work to be done.

635
00:45:44,880 --> 00:45:48,720
Salt water can corrode and just generally destroy.

636
00:45:48,721 --> 00:45:54,100
So as soon as we've been out in saltwater in the ocean, we try and spray it down with water,

637
00:45:54,101 --> 00:45:57,600
give it a good wash, try and get it as clean as possible.

638
00:45:57,601 --> 00:46:01,660
It's not easy to maintain a collection of vehicles from around the world.

639
00:46:01,661 --> 00:46:04,960
Burnett relies on his mechanic, Bruce Simpson.

640
00:46:04,961 --> 00:46:06,627
I'm mechanical by nature.

641
00:46:06,628 --> 00:46:11,160
I joined the Army at 16, did nine years in World Corps of Transport.

642
00:46:11,161 --> 00:46:13,480
When I first started working for them, we had two vehicles.

643
00:46:13,481 --> 00:46:16,020
Now we're up to 37, 38 vehicles.

644
00:46:16,021 --> 00:46:20,220
I'm responsible for finding them and sometimes I'm responsible for getting them into trouble with them.

645
00:46:22,320 --> 00:46:27,260
When Simpson comes across a new prospect, they aren't competing against other collectors.

646
00:46:27,920 --> 00:46:29,080
It's the scrapyard.

647
00:46:29,560 --> 00:46:35,220
Tanks can bring $10,000 or $15,000 just due to the weight of their metal, whether or not they're running.

648
00:46:35,620 --> 00:46:41,100
So I'm always having to bid against people that are going to destroy the vehicle and just to use it for scrap.

649
00:46:41,101 --> 00:46:47,180
Because of the way Burnett utilizes his collection, some of the vehicles end up at the scrap heap anyway.

650
00:46:47,740 --> 00:46:51,240
We ended up losing two vehicles, and the tide came back in, covered them over,

651
00:46:51,340 --> 00:46:54,140
so we had to report them to the Coast Guard as a hazard to navigation.

652
00:46:54,580 --> 00:46:59,120
Eventually, we did get them all winched to shore, although unfortunately, having had that much salt water,

653
00:46:59,540 --> 00:47:01,660
the vehicles were both had to be consigned to the scrap heap.

654
00:47:02,520 --> 00:47:07,900
Burnett's fascination with all the vehicles in his collection lies in their ability to multitask.

655
00:47:07,901 --> 00:47:10,460
It certainly is an element of entertainment there.

656
00:47:10,461 --> 00:47:18,980
They're very big toys, but more than that, they represent a lot of human effort that's gone into engineering a solution to a problem.

657
00:47:19,160 --> 00:47:20,500
I guess that's the thing I like the most.

658
00:47:21,440 --> 00:47:23,380
They can go where others can't.

659
00:47:23,820 --> 00:47:27,740
And for Charles Perneth III, there's no better way to get around.

660
00:47:27,741 --> 00:47:41,220
The first motorized armored fighting vehicles were tractors with metal plates attached

661
00:47:41,221 --> 00:47:43,800
to provide protection to the driver and passenger.

662
00:47:45,660 --> 00:47:49,760
Modern Marvel's private collections will return next on the History Channel.

663
00:47:49,761 --> 00:48:03,760
One of the world's most popular athletes, a golfing legend, Arnold Palmer's golf clubs

664
00:48:03,761 --> 00:48:07,560
are a reminder of some of the game's greatest moments.

665
00:48:07,561 --> 00:48:12,600
It's a collection that's the result of Palmer's pursuit of perfection.

666
00:48:12,601 --> 00:48:19,680
I always felt like if I could design or have built a perfect golf club,

667
00:48:19,681 --> 00:48:22,840
then I could play a perfect game of golf.

668
00:48:22,841 --> 00:48:26,740
Of course, you know and I know that isn't really going to happen,

669
00:48:26,741 --> 00:48:30,820
but the closer you get to doing that,

670
00:48:30,821 --> 00:48:36,360
I always felt like the closer you could get to playing the way you would like to play the game.

671
00:48:38,700 --> 00:48:40,360
Housed in La Trobe, Pennsylvania,

672
00:48:40,361 --> 00:48:43,340
near the course where he first learned to play the game.

673
00:48:44,000 --> 00:48:46,000
Arnold Palmer's ever-evolving collection

674
00:48:46,001 --> 00:48:49,140
contains almost every type of golf club imaginable.

675
00:48:49,920 --> 00:48:51,540
He's used most of them on the course,

676
00:48:51,940 --> 00:48:54,280
and he's applied his own personal touch.

677
00:48:55,260 --> 00:48:59,000
Some people like to read, some people like to do crossword puzzles.

678
00:48:59,080 --> 00:49:00,280
He likes to fill it with golf clubs.

679
00:49:01,200 --> 00:49:04,740
Palmer modifies his golf clubs in an effort to improve his game.

680
00:49:05,400 --> 00:49:06,980
It's part of a daily routine,

681
00:49:07,420 --> 00:49:09,460
and he wouldn't have it any other way.

682
00:49:09,461 --> 00:49:18,500
I spend two, three hours in the morning in the office and get most of my work done.

683
00:49:18,501 --> 00:49:28,140
Then a little time in the shop fiddling with my golf clubs, playing with the golf clubs, fine tuning them, let's call it.

684
00:49:28,860 --> 00:49:36,640
And then the rest of the day on the golf course playing or practicing or doing something that I really enjoy doing.

685
00:49:37,600 --> 00:49:40,520
He absolutely lives, breathes, eats, sleeps, golf.

686
00:49:40,780 --> 00:49:42,280
Every day, that's all he wants to do.

687
00:49:42,400 --> 00:49:46,000
When we have a string of rainy days, it's very difficult for him

688
00:49:46,001 --> 00:49:47,400
because he just wants to get out there and play.

689
00:49:48,160 --> 00:49:51,340
What's good is, in the meantime, he can go back in the shop

690
00:49:51,341 --> 00:49:53,660
and work on some ideas that he's had for a while.

691
00:49:55,440 --> 00:49:57,940
When Palmer turned pro in 1954,

692
00:49:58,560 --> 00:50:03,100
most of the clubs he used did not have the benefits of today's scientific technology.

693
00:50:03,540 --> 00:50:05,240
Although performance was limited,

694
00:50:05,241 --> 00:50:08,240
he still tried to push the technological envelope.

695
00:50:08,241 --> 00:50:11,240
One of the changes that Mr. Palmer did to golf clubs,

696
00:50:11,241 --> 00:50:14,240
I could admit, was either adding or removing weight.

697
00:50:14,241 --> 00:50:18,240
This is a stock McGregor putter with no modifications to it.

698
00:50:18,241 --> 00:50:20,240
This is one where the weight has been added.

699
00:50:20,241 --> 00:50:23,240
You can see he has welded weight on the bottom

700
00:50:23,241 --> 00:50:26,240
and ground it off to give it a nice, smooth feel.

701
00:50:26,241 --> 00:50:29,240
There they are side by side.

702
00:50:29,241 --> 00:50:33,240
Most of the golf tournaments that I've won over the years,

703
00:50:33,241 --> 00:50:37,280
The armor style or the blade putter was the putter I used,

704
00:50:37,660 --> 00:50:42,020
and some of those were probably about 80% weld

705
00:50:42,021 --> 00:50:46,720
because I had ground them down to the point where there was not much left.

706
00:50:47,260 --> 00:50:55,000
I think that that was some of the business that was fun for me.

707
00:50:57,000 --> 00:51:00,820
His shop has all the tools to tinker and alter golf clubs,

708
00:51:00,821 --> 00:51:03,500
including a shaft extractor.

709
00:51:03,900 --> 00:51:05,260
I'll show you how we do this.

710
00:51:05,660 --> 00:51:09,100
We just take this, lock it into place,

711
00:51:10,000 --> 00:51:14,460
and there's an epoxy bond that we use on the graphite.

712
00:51:18,640 --> 00:51:22,560
And I'm careful when I'm heating it not to heat it in one spot too much

713
00:51:22,561 --> 00:51:24,020
because that graphite will just melt.

714
00:51:24,760 --> 00:51:26,320
So I just touch it with a little bit of heat.

715
00:51:26,321 --> 00:51:31,320
That should do it.

716
00:51:31,321 --> 00:51:34,320
And then this is a hydraulic jack.

717
00:51:34,321 --> 00:51:37,320
Just pushes the head right off.

718
00:51:37,321 --> 00:51:39,320
There it is.

719
00:51:39,321 --> 00:51:43,320
And there's the shaft that we can now use and put in a new club.

720
00:51:43,321 --> 00:51:46,320
Another tool is the iron bending machine.

721
00:51:46,321 --> 00:51:50,320
It's utilized to change the lie angle of the club or adjust the loft.

722
00:51:50,321 --> 00:51:55,320
So what we do with this is grab a bending bar.

723
00:51:55,321 --> 00:52:01,340
Lock it on the hosel and you can bend this however you need to to get it to the right

724
00:52:01,341 --> 00:52:02,340
lie and loft.

725
00:52:02,341 --> 00:52:06,940
And something that's interesting with Mr. Palmer is he can take a look at this club

726
00:52:06,941 --> 00:52:10,360
once he pulls it out of the machine, set it on the ground and say, well that needs to

727
00:52:10,361 --> 00:52:16,500
be a half a degree higher in the lie angle.

728
00:52:16,501 --> 00:52:18,320
He doesn't even really use these gauges.

729
00:52:18,321 --> 00:52:22,220
The gauges are there for the novices like me when we're doing club work and we need

730
00:52:22,221 --> 00:52:23,420
to have a number to go by.

731
00:52:23,421 --> 00:52:27,540
But he'll just be able to eyeball that and tell it if it's where it needs to be.

732
00:52:28,700 --> 00:52:33,320
Palmer has won 92 tournaments in a career that spans more than half a century.

733
00:52:33,980 --> 00:52:36,640
But with all the experimenting Palmer's done over the years,

734
00:52:36,980 --> 00:52:40,920
it's not easy to recall which clubs were making golfing history.

735
00:52:41,400 --> 00:52:45,720
We're asked constantly if we have, say, the putter that Mr. Palmer used

736
00:52:45,721 --> 00:52:48,220
when he won the 1960 Masters.

737
00:52:48,221 --> 00:52:54,400
The problem is he's constantly switching and changing clubs.

738
00:52:54,620 --> 00:52:58,140
I mean, he's actually won tournaments where he's used a different set of irons

739
00:52:58,141 --> 00:53:00,140
or a different driver every day of the round.

740
00:53:00,960 --> 00:53:04,520
During practice, he selects his clubs based on how they feel,

741
00:53:05,080 --> 00:53:09,060
and Palmer's always mindful of his first stroke off the tee.

742
00:53:09,680 --> 00:53:11,380
He's always looking for a little extra length.

743
00:53:11,820 --> 00:53:13,280
You know, that's the main criteria.

744
00:53:13,640 --> 00:53:16,320
Some of them may be a little bit more consistently accurate,

745
00:53:16,321 --> 00:53:20,160
but length is what he's primarily concerned with on his drivers.

746
00:53:20,800 --> 00:53:23,920
Even the most consistent parts of his game sometimes fail him.

747
00:53:24,580 --> 00:53:28,860
When Palmer doesn't play well, his army of fans, known as Arnie's Army,

748
00:53:29,260 --> 00:53:31,420
do their part to aid their favorite golfer.

749
00:53:31,860 --> 00:53:33,880
All he used to have to do when he was playing in a tournament

750
00:53:33,881 --> 00:53:37,880
was have a complaint about his putting printed in the newspaper

751
00:53:37,881 --> 00:53:42,040
and immediately clubs would come in from fans saying,

752
00:53:42,041 --> 00:53:44,420
well, I read about your putting problems.

753
00:53:44,421 --> 00:53:45,980
I do great with this putter of mine.

754
00:53:46,100 --> 00:53:47,540
I'd like you to have it and try it.

755
00:53:47,620 --> 00:53:48,560
I think it'll help your putting.

756
00:53:49,320 --> 00:53:52,660
Putters, sent by fans, may not have improved his game,

757
00:53:52,860 --> 00:53:55,020
but they sure make for a colorful collection.

758
00:53:55,580 --> 00:53:59,320
Golf clubs that have been sent to me over the years

759
00:53:59,321 --> 00:54:01,900
go from rocket ships to bananas.

760
00:54:02,360 --> 00:54:04,480
And you think that may be an exaggeration.

761
00:54:04,600 --> 00:54:05,300
It's not.

762
00:54:05,600 --> 00:54:07,600
Some of the more fun putters that we've received,

763
00:54:07,900 --> 00:54:10,600
not just one, but four people came up with the idea

764
00:54:10,601 --> 00:54:13,500
of using hammers as heads for putters.

765
00:54:14,420 --> 00:54:16,360
We also have the whole line of foods.

766
00:54:16,520 --> 00:54:18,280
We have one made out of a piece of corn.

767
00:54:19,580 --> 00:54:23,160
There's a fish, a hot dog, and a banana.

768
00:54:24,580 --> 00:54:28,320
We also have one that looks like a Campbell's soup can.

769
00:54:28,960 --> 00:54:32,280
And from his friends at Pennzoil, of course we have one that's a Pennzoil bottle.

770
00:54:33,860 --> 00:54:38,960
There are 2,000 putters and more than 10,000 clubs in Palmer's collection.

771
00:54:39,740 --> 00:54:43,980
If I have 10,000 golf clubs, I'd give 10,000 away, too.

772
00:54:44,420 --> 00:54:50,020
A lot of the golf clubs have been sent to military guys that were overseas.

773
00:54:50,700 --> 00:54:52,640
Some clubs have been given to pros.

774
00:54:53,380 --> 00:54:58,000
One in particular helped a fellow golfer named Tony Lima in 1964.

775
00:54:58,720 --> 00:55:03,420
I suggested to him that he go play in the British Open at St. Andrews.

776
00:55:04,000 --> 00:55:08,800
And after a little grilling and pumping, he said,

777
00:55:08,860 --> 00:55:13,300
OK, Arnie, he says, I will go to the British Open, but he says, I need your caddy.

778
00:55:14,420 --> 00:55:15,300
And your putter.

779
00:55:16,660 --> 00:55:18,060
And I said, okay.

780
00:55:18,480 --> 00:55:19,800
And he won the British Open.

781
00:55:20,320 --> 00:55:25,600
I got more of a kick out of that than a lot of things that I've done in my life.

782
00:55:26,800 --> 00:55:31,980
Even after 70 years on the course, Arnie is as serious as ever about golf.

783
00:55:32,840 --> 00:55:38,260
As long as he's playing and experimenting, his collection of clubs will continue to grow.

784
00:55:39,120 --> 00:55:44,060
I'm one of the people that always look to the next day and say,

785
00:55:44,061 --> 00:55:51,180
well, you know, maybe I'll find something that will make this old guy play a little bit better golf tomorrow.

786
00:55:52,240 --> 00:55:56,520
For Arnold Palmer, the collection reflects the best part of his golfing life,

787
00:55:56,780 --> 00:56:01,320
a collection of tools with which he crafted some of golf's greatest moments.

788
00:56:04,320 --> 00:56:09,520
The most sought-after clubs are the early 1400s Long Nose Woods.

789
00:56:10,480 --> 00:56:15,600
Much like a modern-day ice hockey stick, they were commonly used for over 400 years.

790
00:56:16,200 --> 00:56:21,200
Today, they are valued at anywhere between $3,650 and $100,000.

791
00:56:24,220 --> 00:56:26,580
He was the king of rock and roll.

792
00:56:27,240 --> 00:56:32,920
He drove expensive cars, wore fine clothes, and sported the finest jewelry money could buy.

793
00:56:33,260 --> 00:56:39,220
His everlasting stardom makes anything he possessed the most sought-after of all collectibles.

794
00:56:39,520 --> 00:56:46,200
There are so many people infatuated with Elvis because Elvis transcends all races and religions.

795
00:56:46,660 --> 00:56:50,120
Billed as the world's greatest Elvis collection outside of Graceland,

796
00:56:50,700 --> 00:56:55,200
owner Chris Davidson has brought together memorabilia from the superstar's famous career.

797
00:56:55,820 --> 00:57:00,080
From blue suede shoes to Cadillac limousines, from jewelry to jumpsuits,

798
00:57:00,081 --> 00:57:03,120
if Elvis had it, Chris Davidson bought it.

799
00:57:03,600 --> 00:57:08,300
And it's all housed in the famed city where Elvis performed the most, Las Vegas.

800
00:57:09,520 --> 00:57:16,960
Everybody expects to see something Elvis related when they come to Vegas.

801
00:57:17,380 --> 00:57:23,440
Elvis is most closely associated with the city because Vegas is a late night town and Elvis was a late night guy.

802
00:57:24,340 --> 00:57:28,120
Chris Davidson's infatuation with Elvis began long ago.

803
00:57:28,121 --> 00:57:29,939
I saw Elvis twice as a kid.

804
00:57:29,940 --> 00:57:35,600
Once here in Las Vegas when I was about 12 and once again in Long Beach, California.

805
00:57:35,940 --> 00:57:38,320
After I saw him the first time, I was completely hooked.

806
00:57:38,321 --> 00:57:43,320
When purchasing a new collectible, sentimental value is also important.

807
00:57:43,321 --> 00:57:46,320
I actually like to collect things of Elvis' that meant a lot to him,

808
00:57:46,321 --> 00:57:49,320
which don't always necessarily relate to money.

809
00:57:49,321 --> 00:57:53,320
I have a piece that he gave to his father that's a poem, basically,

810
00:57:53,321 --> 00:57:57,320
that talks about how he's been to the top of the mountain and that he's had enough.

811
00:57:57,321 --> 00:58:01,320
Davidson turned his admiration into a $5 million collection

812
00:58:01,321 --> 00:58:04,320
that spans every era of Elvis' career.

813
00:58:04,321 --> 00:58:09,167
his career. I bought my first Elvis piece at the 94 Las Vegas Hilton auction.

814
00:58:09,168 --> 00:58:10,300
It just went crazy

815
00:58:10,301 --> 00:58:16,240
from there. I just continued to buy items until I ran out of money.

816
00:58:16,241 --> 00:58:18,900
In 1954, Elvis recorded That's

817
00:58:18,901 --> 00:58:22,969
Alright, the first of five singles released by Sun Records.

818
00:58:22,970 --> 00:58:25,520
Destined for success, he hit the road

819
00:58:25,521 --> 00:58:30,765
playing small clubs throughout the South. This was Elvis' 1955 Cadillac limousine.

820
00:58:30,766 --> 00:58:31,660
Elvis slept in

821
00:58:31,661 --> 00:58:35,640
the back, he drove it, they put their equipment up on top. It's a very cool car.

822
00:58:36,800 --> 00:58:42,120
Elvis purchased the limo with the money he received when Sun Studios sold his record contract to RCA.

823
00:58:43,280 --> 00:58:46,400
Another car in the collection is Elvis' black Mercedes.

824
00:58:47,100 --> 00:58:49,200
I'm in that car, a little over $300,000.

825
00:58:49,201 --> 00:58:52,140
It was purchased originally at auction at the first sale

826
00:58:52,141 --> 00:58:57,740
that I went to in 94, and it is the most expensive thing that I've ever paid for.

827
00:58:57,741 --> 00:59:00,340
But cars weren't Elvis' only love.

828
00:59:00,341 --> 00:59:07,340
In January 1956, Elvis recorded a song named after a really cool pair of shoes.

829
00:59:07,341 --> 00:59:10,540
Elvis soon added a pair to his wardrobe on stage.

830
00:59:10,541 --> 00:59:15,340
The prized blue suede shoes are valued at $1 million.

831
00:59:15,341 --> 00:59:18,140
It didn't matter what Elvis wore.

832
00:59:18,141 --> 00:59:21,140
There was total pandemonium whenever he performed.

833
00:59:21,141 --> 00:59:26,740
Girls loved the way he swiveled his hips, while parents claimed the country's morals were eroding.

834
00:59:26,741 --> 00:59:33,740
But in 1958, Elvis entered a new era in his life that drastically altered more than his wardrobe.

835
00:59:33,741 --> 00:59:37,740
Going into the Army was really both scary and huge for Elvis.

836
00:59:37,741 --> 00:59:41,740
Elvis wanted to be a good soldier and to do his part with America,

837
00:59:41,741 --> 00:59:47,740
but certainly very scary for him because he wasn't sure where his career would be when he came back.

838
00:59:47,741 --> 00:59:51,740
The collection contains several items from Elvis' years in the military.

839
00:59:51,741 --> 00:59:55,740
Here's the Army display case. There are some fabulous pieces here in this case.

840
00:59:55,741 --> 00:59:57,720
here in this case, inside this

841
00:59:57,721 --> 00:59:59,760
army trunk, which was shipped by Elvis

842
00:59:59,761 --> 01:00:01,660
from Germany to Memphis, were

843
01:00:01,661 --> 01:00:03,600
the fatigues, which I also purchased from the

844
01:00:03,601 --> 01:00:05,640
Graceland auction. There were many

845
01:00:05,641 --> 01:00:07,700
documents related to Elvis'

846
01:00:07,920 --> 01:00:09,860
stay in Germany. After he finished

847
01:00:09,861 --> 01:00:11,740
serving his country, Elvis wore

848
01:00:11,741 --> 01:00:13,960
this military coat on more than one occasion.

849
01:00:14,440 --> 01:00:15,740
Elvis did a film called G.I.

850
01:00:15,800 --> 01:00:17,360
Blues as soon as he got out of the army,

851
01:00:17,820 --> 01:00:19,600
and I purchased it from John

852
01:00:19,601 --> 01:00:21,900
Torag, who is the son of director Norm Torag,

853
01:00:21,901 --> 01:00:23,960
who directed nine different Elvis Presley

854
01:00:23,961 --> 01:00:26,293
films. In November 1960, G.I.

855
01:00:26,294 --> 01:00:30,960
Blues opened nationally to warm reviews and big box office

856
01:00:30,961 --> 01:00:36,200
sales. Elvis had more money than he ever dreamed and free time to do as he pleased.

857
01:00:36,201 --> 01:00:41,123
Behind us is Elvis' boat. Elvis enjoyed boating up and down the Mississippi.

858
01:00:41,124 --> 01:00:41,900
He had a lot

859
01:00:41,901 --> 01:00:48,740
of downtime in between films in the 60s, and so he and the guys would go out and go skiing

860
01:00:48,741 --> 01:00:55,240
Although he remained dedicated to his film career, Elvis longed to perform back on the stage.

861
01:00:56,200 --> 01:01:01,980
In 1969, he returned to one of the few places he hadn't been a hit yet, Las Vegas.

862
01:01:02,760 --> 01:01:06,880
Elvis first performed in the city in 1956 to Luke Warm Response.

863
01:01:07,640 --> 01:01:11,740
Upon his return, he followed the advice of Las Vegas veteran Liberace.

864
01:01:12,600 --> 01:01:16,740
Liberace's advice to him was, you'll be very memorable the more glitzy and glamorous that you are,

865
01:01:16,741 --> 01:01:21,580
which of course Liberace was, and of course Elvis really in the 70s developed and honed.

866
01:01:22,540 --> 01:01:26,640
Elvis stormed the stage wearing outrageous jumpsuits and fine jewelry.

867
01:01:27,140 --> 01:01:29,360
And whenever Chris Davidson wants a touch of Elvis,

868
01:01:29,840 --> 01:01:32,720
he adorns himself with the king's personal possessions.

869
01:01:33,200 --> 01:01:35,460
Jewelry is really my greatest fascination.

870
01:01:35,880 --> 01:01:38,080
I have many pieces that I own that are fabulous.

871
01:01:38,340 --> 01:01:40,940
The 42-carat Red Star Ruby ring is stunning.

872
01:01:41,520 --> 01:01:44,980
Elvis paid 75 grand for this ring and gave it to a friend of his.

873
01:01:44,981 --> 01:01:47,720
And I paid around 25 grand for the ring.

874
01:01:48,520 --> 01:01:51,720
Each piece of jewelry in the collection is a rare one-of-a-kind.

875
01:01:52,680 --> 01:01:57,080
I also have a black medallion with Elvis spelled out in diamonds, which is very cool.

876
01:01:57,800 --> 01:02:03,720
Although Elvis liked expensive jewelry, he was more interested in obtaining items related to his own personal safety.

877
01:02:04,240 --> 01:02:09,080
In the 50s, when he did his performances, they would need to hire for extra security police officers.

878
01:02:09,081 --> 01:02:16,440
And Elvis became fascinated with guns and badges, and so over the years collected a vast amount of badges and guns,

879
01:02:16,820 --> 01:02:20,800
with the ultimate badge being the DEA badge that he received from President Nixon.

880
01:02:21,260 --> 01:02:27,860
At Elvis' written request, he met President Nixon in the White House to discuss the nation's widespread drug abuse.

881
01:02:28,640 --> 01:02:33,420
Nixon gave him a special agent's badge, which Chris Davidson purchased for $10,000.

882
01:02:33,421 --> 01:02:39,660
Ironically, it was Elvis' own use of prescription pills that would take a toll on him.

883
01:02:40,140 --> 01:02:45,560
On August 16, 1977, at 42 years of age, Elvis Presley died.

884
01:02:46,140 --> 01:02:48,820
Despite his death, his popularity has never faded.

885
01:02:49,300 --> 01:02:53,700
The reason that fans refused to let Elvis go is just because he was such a significant part of their life.

886
01:02:54,420 --> 01:02:57,240
Elvis was almost like a religious figure to many people.

887
01:02:57,580 --> 01:03:02,700
Elvis is actually the number two recognized image in the world behind Mickey Mouse.

888
01:03:03,960 --> 01:03:06,620
Elvis will always be the king of rock and roll.

889
01:03:06,820 --> 01:03:11,320
And Chris Davidson does not see the value of his collection diminishing anytime soon.

890
01:03:11,780 --> 01:03:14,780
I've gone both ways with trying to, you know, decide the future.

891
01:03:14,781 --> 01:03:17,060
I mean, we have several million dollars invested here.

892
01:03:17,880 --> 01:03:20,520
But ultimately, I believe Elvis will stand the test of time.

893
01:03:20,980 --> 01:03:23,160
He's in the hearts of millions of fans.

894
01:03:23,620 --> 01:03:27,520
And with collectors like Chris Davidson taking care of his most cherished mementos,

895
01:03:28,060 --> 01:03:30,680
Elvis is sure to live on forever.

896
01:03:34,420 --> 01:03:37,720
The very first guitar Elvis Presley owned at the age of 11

897
01:03:38,160 --> 01:03:42,880
was purchased at the Tupelo Hardware Store in Mississippi for $7.75.

898
01:03:43,420 --> 01:03:46,920
Today, the guitar is valued at around $1 million.

899
01:03:47,820 --> 01:03:52,120
Modern Marvel's private collections will return next on the History Channel.

900
01:03:55,620 --> 01:03:58,520
It's one of the finest sports collections in Hollywood today.

901
01:04:00,120 --> 01:04:02,520
And it belongs to one of our finest leading ladies.

902
01:04:03,420 --> 01:04:07,420
Acclaimed actor and director Penny Marshall.

903
01:04:07,421 --> 01:04:10,420
The star of the Emmy Award-winning show Laverne & Shirley.

904
01:04:10,421 --> 01:04:14,420
And director of the blockbusters Big and A League of Their Own.

905
01:04:14,421 --> 01:04:17,420
She loves everything about sports.

906
01:04:20,420 --> 01:04:23,420
Penny has the artifacts from the glory days.

907
01:04:23,421 --> 01:04:27,420
The tangible evidence of the incredible moments gone by.

908
01:04:27,421 --> 01:04:30,420
And the relationships with superstar athletes.

909
01:04:30,421 --> 01:04:33,220
to make each item more than a keepsake.

910
01:04:34,560 --> 01:04:36,340
Born in the shadow of Yankee Stadium,

911
01:04:36,800 --> 01:04:38,920
Penny's been a sports fan all her life.

912
01:04:41,380 --> 01:04:43,220
I'm from the Bronx, so we cut school

913
01:04:43,221 --> 01:04:45,700
and go to Yankee games and see Maris and Manila.

914
01:04:46,840 --> 01:04:48,580
We took the bus by the subway.

915
01:04:49,140 --> 01:04:51,220
The last thing you see before it goes underground

916
01:04:51,221 --> 01:04:53,120
is that slice of Yankee Stadium,

917
01:04:53,920 --> 01:04:55,500
which is the most beautiful view.

918
01:04:56,840 --> 01:04:59,760
From an early age, she had a collector's instinct.

919
01:05:00,340 --> 01:05:02,800
When I was little, I had baseball cards.

920
01:05:03,140 --> 01:05:06,240
I always collected something but not to the six.

921
01:05:06,580 --> 01:05:08,160
As you get older, you get nuts.

922
01:05:08,560 --> 01:05:10,880
A struggling actress when she first came to Hollywood,

923
01:05:11,260 --> 01:05:13,580
her collecting was limited by a lack of funds.

924
01:05:14,140 --> 01:05:16,000
But with the success of Laverne and Shirley,

925
01:05:16,001 --> 01:05:18,560
she was able to start her dream collection.

926
01:05:20,800 --> 01:05:23,840
Her collectibles are divided into separate departments,

927
01:05:24,220 --> 01:05:25,580
each featuring a different sport.

928
01:05:26,140 --> 01:05:28,360
The older items are often the most valuable.

929
01:05:28,361 --> 01:05:30,600
Baseball, history of our country.

930
01:05:30,601 --> 01:05:33,860
It was an American game, so I like that.

931
01:05:38,240 --> 01:05:40,820
This is Negro Leagues,

932
01:05:41,200 --> 01:05:43,900
and those are from the Negro League line balls.

933
01:05:45,140 --> 01:05:48,000
Part of the collection isn't about the stars on the field,

934
01:05:48,280 --> 01:05:49,820
but the fans in the seats.

935
01:05:50,280 --> 01:05:51,600
Now, those are from the polo grounds.

936
01:05:52,460 --> 01:05:54,380
That's Yankee Stadium, the brown one.

937
01:05:55,440 --> 01:05:57,040
That's from Ebbets Field.

938
01:05:58,360 --> 01:06:04,040
When a lucky fan catches a souvenir baseball, it's his dream to add the batter's autograph.

939
01:06:04,760 --> 01:06:08,420
These are Yankees, and these are the Yankee World Series.

940
01:06:09,080 --> 01:06:16,220
I like this ball because it's a year of my birth, but we're not showing it up close because it's none of your business.

941
01:06:16,620 --> 01:06:18,000
I like the old-timers.

942
01:06:18,160 --> 01:06:20,560
Satchel Paige, Walter Johnson, those.

943
01:06:20,640 --> 01:06:23,280
Here's Michael Jordan's baseball.

944
01:06:23,500 --> 01:06:24,600
I lost that for three years.

945
01:06:24,660 --> 01:06:25,980
It was in the garage in a box.

946
01:06:25,981 --> 01:06:32,240
One prized possession Penny couldn't pass up is this memento of her Yankee hero, Jolton Joe DiMaggio.

947
01:06:33,100 --> 01:06:37,600
In 1941, the Yankee Clippers set a record that may never be broken.

948
01:06:38,100 --> 01:06:42,860
This is a table someone made when DiMaggio went on his 56 hitting streak.

949
01:06:43,120 --> 01:06:46,380
And so there's 56 signed Joe DiMaggio balls in here.

950
01:06:47,060 --> 01:06:49,580
Her bat rack reads like a Hall of Fame roster.

951
01:06:50,260 --> 01:06:54,160
And there's even an award to the player known as Charlie Hustle, Pete Rose.

952
01:06:54,161 --> 01:06:58,160
This is one of my favorite things. It's for Pete Rose.

953
01:06:58,460 --> 01:07:06,220
Baseball writers of Association of America presented Pete Rose with an award for exemplary conduct on and off the field.

954
01:07:07,120 --> 01:07:09,640
But Penny is also a big round ball fan.

955
01:07:10,380 --> 01:07:15,180
An NBA season ticket holder, she's friends with many basketball superstars.

956
01:07:16,180 --> 01:07:23,180
In the basketball section, I have balls that are from the 1999-2000 season.

957
01:07:23,181 --> 01:07:28,780
season because i have i lived at the time of a century penny even drafts players to help get the

958
01:07:28,781 --> 01:07:34,700
rest of the team's signatures i sit near the visiting team and i usually know someone on it

959
01:07:35,340 --> 01:07:39,900
and so i'd see who was injured like a long time morning was injured that year and i said um

960
01:07:40,540 --> 01:07:45,900
i need this sign and every time she she says i'm getting it i'm getting it i swear

961
01:07:45,901 --> 01:07:52,860
And so each player from each team got reassigned ball from their team.

962
01:07:53,400 --> 01:07:59,540
After finally receiving the basketballs, Penny spent hours trying to identify and label the scribbled signatures.

963
01:08:00,020 --> 01:08:02,160
You can't read these words darn.

964
01:08:03,060 --> 01:08:07,720
Now, so if they write their number, you bless them.

965
01:08:08,720 --> 01:08:12,600
Like all sports collectors, she is wary of fraudulent merchandise,

966
01:08:12,601 --> 01:08:18,540
usually demanding a certificate of authenticity when purchasing autographed items but even the

967
01:08:18,541 --> 01:08:25,500
most cautious of collectors can still be tricked I've got this with a certificate and Colby was

968
01:08:25,501 --> 01:08:30,720
up here and I said oh yeah I got both signed by you he said that's not my signature I said Colby

969
01:08:30,721 --> 01:08:37,260
it came with the thing he said it's not my signature but it came with the piece of paper

970
01:08:37,261 --> 01:08:40,500
but that's it. So, he re-signed it.

971
01:08:41,820 --> 01:08:44,460
When Penny participated in a charity event,

972
01:08:44,461 --> 01:08:49,100
she received Shaquille O'Neal's size 22 shoes and other collectibles.

973
01:08:49,101 --> 01:08:53,100
This is Shaq's shoe, which is facing the wrong way.

974
01:08:53,101 --> 01:08:56,500
Shaq Amy, because I do a Shaq-Tacular event.

975
01:08:56,501 --> 01:08:59,860
This my daughter got me.

976
01:08:59,861 --> 01:09:02,100
This is a Michael Jordan phone.

977
01:09:02,101 --> 01:09:04,940
One, two, three.

978
01:09:04,941 --> 01:09:07,220
For you Chicago fans.

979
01:09:08,080 --> 01:09:14,720
Other superstar players in the basketball section include Magic, Bird, and Barkley.

980
01:09:15,060 --> 01:09:21,840
The unusual one-of-a-kind Dennis Rodman doll is one of several collectibles of basketball's bad boys.

981
01:09:22,140 --> 01:09:24,740
Dennis throws me the ball in the middle.

982
01:09:25,920 --> 01:09:29,340
I went back and at the end he gave me the shirt.

983
01:09:29,341 --> 01:09:32,240
So I went back and met him, and he signed it.

984
01:09:32,320 --> 01:09:33,800
That's what it says to Miss Marshall.

985
01:09:34,340 --> 01:09:35,320
He's got the best heart.

986
01:09:35,900 --> 01:09:38,500
Dennis, he really is a good-hearted person.

987
01:09:39,120 --> 01:09:41,880
Some of her memorabilia pack a powerful punch.

988
01:09:42,740 --> 01:09:43,680
You gotta get Ali.

989
01:09:44,000 --> 01:09:46,000
It's Ali's pants on top.

990
01:09:46,200 --> 01:09:48,040
Then we head into football.

991
01:09:49,060 --> 01:09:51,420
So Montana, I directed in a commercial.

992
01:09:52,740 --> 01:09:55,040
Walter Payton, Sweetness, and Gail Sayer.

993
01:09:55,620 --> 01:09:57,520
Jim Kelly, Steve Young.

994
01:09:58,400 --> 01:10:00,680
Through her brother, film director Gary Marshall,

995
01:10:01,020 --> 01:10:03,680
she met one of her sports heroes on the set of a movie.

996
01:10:04,260 --> 01:10:07,720
Johnny Unitas was there, and so he signed a football to me.

997
01:10:07,900 --> 01:10:09,940
So it's nice when you meet them personally.

998
01:10:10,140 --> 01:10:11,560
I mean, he was a great player.

999
01:10:12,420 --> 01:10:16,100
One of the most unique parts of the collection is the music library.

1000
01:10:16,720 --> 01:10:18,660
These are all sheet music.

1001
01:10:20,280 --> 01:10:24,880
To every baseball song ever written.

1002
01:10:24,881 --> 01:10:27,620
So these are old.

1003
01:10:28,860 --> 01:10:30,000
Old, old, old.

1004
01:10:30,120 --> 01:10:31,780
I'm trying to see a date on something.

1005
01:10:32,520 --> 01:10:33,220
1885.

1006
01:10:35,320 --> 01:10:39,080
So I think it's important that they know this team is formed.

1007
01:10:39,760 --> 01:10:43,940
For all her love of sports, Penny regrets she never really participated.

1008
01:10:44,320 --> 01:10:46,140
I wish I could have played sports.

1009
01:10:47,140 --> 01:10:50,220
I wasn't quite encouraged when I was growing up.

1010
01:10:50,660 --> 01:10:52,320
It wasn't something you were supposed to do.

1011
01:10:52,321 --> 01:10:55,280
Girls weren't supposed to play ball.

1012
01:10:56,720 --> 01:11:00,940
In 1991, Penny directed the hit movie A League of Their Own,

1013
01:11:01,260 --> 01:11:04,420
the story of the American Girls Professional Baseball League.

1014
01:11:04,920 --> 01:11:09,240
When filming was completed, Penny's team presented her with some cherished mementos.

1015
01:11:09,820 --> 01:11:11,500
They had the bat, the cast sign.

1016
01:11:12,380 --> 01:11:14,740
There's a ball over there, the crew sign.

1017
01:11:14,920 --> 01:11:21,360
The cast made that shirt up there with all the pictures on it for just the crew members.

1018
01:11:22,320 --> 01:11:23,660
And they signed that.

1019
01:11:24,300 --> 01:11:28,380
Each piece of the collection has been bought, begged, or bequeathed.

1020
01:11:28,420 --> 01:11:33,320
But one of the most prized pieces came, one might say, from out of left field.

1021
01:11:33,940 --> 01:11:38,120
This is Faye Dancer, who Madonna's character is based on.

1022
01:11:38,480 --> 01:11:43,140
These are some of the real players from the All-American Girls Baseball League.

1023
01:11:43,141 --> 01:11:47,300
Now, this was sent to me by Garth Brooks, who I met once.

1024
01:11:48,080 --> 01:11:50,160
And he had bought it in a charity auction.

1025
01:11:50,161 --> 01:11:52,920
and he said, I thought you'd appreciate this.

1026
01:11:53,600 --> 01:11:55,040
And it was so sweet of him.

1027
01:11:55,460 --> 01:11:58,120
I mean, this is someone who I don't really know.

1028
01:11:59,420 --> 01:12:01,160
Penny's collection is very personal

1029
01:12:01,161 --> 01:12:04,300
since she's chosen items that have special meaning to her.

1030
01:12:05,060 --> 01:12:06,740
These are the best at what they do

1031
01:12:06,741 --> 01:12:11,120
and so I like to bask in the glory of others, I guess.

1032
01:12:14,000 --> 01:12:17,520
A mint 1909 Honus Wagner baseball card

1033
01:12:17,521 --> 01:12:19,120
is worth over $1 million.

1034
01:12:19,121 --> 01:12:22,700
Legend has it so few Wagner cards were made

1035
01:12:22,701 --> 01:12:27,220
because he refused to have his name associated with a distributor, a tobacco company.

1036
01:12:28,120 --> 01:12:32,280
Modern Marvel's private collections will return next on the History Channel.

1037
01:12:35,520 --> 01:12:36,980
Star Wars.

1038
01:12:37,480 --> 01:12:41,380
A science fiction adventure that captured the world's imagination

1039
01:12:41,381 --> 01:12:44,960
and created an unprecedented merchandising phenomenon.

1040
01:12:45,860 --> 01:12:48,520
The successful Hollywood fantasy spawned sequels,

1041
01:12:49,120 --> 01:12:52,260
prequels, and billions of dollars in movie merchandise

1042
01:12:52,500 --> 01:12:55,840
that would become some of the most sought-after collectibles on the planet.

1043
01:12:57,960 --> 01:13:02,260
One man's passion for the groundbreaking film has driven him to scour the Earth

1044
01:13:02,300 --> 01:13:04,960
to acquire the largest and most valuable collection

1045
01:13:05,300 --> 01:13:07,720
of Star Wars memorabilia in existence.

1046
01:13:08,720 --> 01:13:13,060
To think that I would have a 5,000-square-foot barn filled with a Star Wars collection,

1047
01:13:13,440 --> 01:13:15,900
no, not in my wildest imagination.

1048
01:13:19,120 --> 01:13:23,040
Steve has well over a hundred thousand pieces in his remarkable collection.

1049
01:13:23,520 --> 01:13:30,280
It's made up of toys, posters, trading cards, action figures, and movie props.

1050
01:13:30,980 --> 01:13:35,780
His collection is irresistible and can't help bring out the kid in anyone.

1051
01:13:38,140 --> 01:13:40,280
Would you believe this is a real lightsaber?

1052
01:13:40,540 --> 01:13:43,860
Well, not exactly. There aren't such things as real lightsabers.

1053
01:13:44,200 --> 01:13:47,540
This is a great prop replica from a company called Master Replicas.

1054
01:13:47,541 --> 01:13:49,600
and you can actually make it work.

1055
01:13:49,800 --> 01:13:51,040
Now, back up, please.

1056
01:13:51,520 --> 01:13:52,680
This is only for pros.

1057
01:13:59,820 --> 01:14:00,700
Cool, huh?

1058
01:14:01,360 --> 01:14:03,200
But not everything is a toy.

1059
01:14:03,860 --> 01:14:07,600
Some of the more valuable pieces are to look at and not to play with.

1060
01:14:08,000 --> 01:14:10,440
Some of these rare items were hard to find.

1061
01:14:11,120 --> 01:14:14,180
So here is the prototype R2-D2 lunch pail,

1062
01:14:14,181 --> 01:14:17,580
the vinyl lunch pail that I searched for for 10 years and finally found.

1063
01:14:18,300 --> 01:14:24,280
Right over here is a very rare Boba Fett action figure with the missile firing mechanism still in it.

1064
01:14:24,400 --> 01:14:26,780
That was never released because of product safety issues.

1065
01:14:28,000 --> 01:14:32,420
Row after row, shelf after shelf of Star Wars memorabilia.

1066
01:14:32,880 --> 01:14:37,480
There are items from around the world, and everything in the collection has one thing in common.

1067
01:14:37,980 --> 01:14:39,320
No two are alike.

1068
01:14:39,321 --> 01:14:44,460
This model is actually the three-dimensional blueprint of the full-size Millennium Falcon

1069
01:14:44,461 --> 01:14:49,160
that was built 60 feet in diameter for Empire Strikes Back and Return of the Jedi.

1070
01:14:50,760 --> 01:14:55,580
And this is a kit that was built for me by a friend from Germany.

1071
01:14:56,540 --> 01:15:00,960
He researched every single piece that they used to make the original Millennium Falcon.

1072
01:15:01,420 --> 01:15:03,300
More than 2,400 pieces.

1073
01:15:03,500 --> 01:15:06,840
All the model kits they bashed and came up with this amazing model.

1074
01:15:07,840 --> 01:15:12,400
3,000 movie posters, practically every book about Star Wars ever written,

1075
01:15:12,660 --> 01:15:14,860
and clothing in every size.

1076
01:15:16,160 --> 01:15:19,040
It all began with the movie's release in 1977.

1077
01:15:19,940 --> 01:15:23,840
Little did Steve Sansweet know, it was the beginning of a Star Wars obsession.

1078
01:15:26,560 --> 01:15:28,460
Why does any collector collect?

1079
01:15:28,920 --> 01:15:30,080
It's the passion.

1080
01:15:30,520 --> 01:15:32,280
The movie blew me away.

1081
01:15:32,840 --> 01:15:35,160
My background in loving science fiction,

1082
01:15:35,161 --> 01:15:37,160
and I thought, this is the be-all, end-all.

1083
01:15:37,280 --> 01:15:39,180
This is a movie that was entertaining.

1084
01:15:39,600 --> 01:15:40,620
It was fun.

1085
01:15:41,000 --> 01:15:46,200
I connected with it in a way I sort of related to the story of Luke Skywalker

1086
01:15:46,201 --> 01:15:47,760
and his yearning to leave home.

1087
01:15:51,180 --> 01:15:52,920
Not long after seeing the movie,

1088
01:15:53,280 --> 01:15:55,900
Steve began competing with kids at toy stores

1089
01:15:55,901 --> 01:15:58,120
for the latest Star Wars memorabilia.

1090
01:15:58,380 --> 01:16:02,580
The great thing about being on the ground floor of what has become a collectible

1091
01:16:02,581 --> 01:16:06,020
is the ability to go in the store and buy something for $1.97

1092
01:16:06,021 --> 01:16:09,680
that years later can be worth a lot more than $1.97.

1093
01:16:10,120 --> 01:16:13,200
One of the very first movies to widely market merchandise,

1094
01:16:13,860 --> 01:16:17,800
no one knew for sure what Star Wars items would sell to the buying public.

1095
01:16:18,480 --> 01:16:21,300
One of the first items produced was a Chewbacca mug.

1096
01:16:21,880 --> 01:16:25,600
The character was inspired by George Lucas' dog, Indiana.

1097
01:16:27,200 --> 01:16:30,420
With every sequel, there was more and more to buy,

1098
01:16:30,421 --> 01:16:33,880
from miniature action figures to life-size characters.

1099
01:16:34,880 --> 01:16:38,140
So behind me is one of the icons of the Star Wars universe,

1100
01:16:38,300 --> 01:16:39,300
the Han and Carbonite.

1101
01:16:39,460 --> 01:16:43,100
And right next to him, of course, is the bounty hunter who captured Han, Boba Fett.

1102
01:16:43,300 --> 01:16:47,360
Both of these are prop replicas and really nicely made.

1103
01:16:50,220 --> 01:16:54,120
This, the Stormtrooper, is the character that everybody wants to be.

1104
01:16:54,200 --> 01:16:56,720
This is another prop replica from Don Post Studios.

1105
01:16:57,620 --> 01:17:02,180
Another full-size character is everybody's favorite droid, R2-D2.

1106
01:17:02,460 --> 01:17:06,200
R2 always got our heroes out of trouble.

1107
01:17:06,680 --> 01:17:12,460
R2 was both a figure with someone inside to operate and move it back and forth.

1108
01:17:12,840 --> 01:17:16,920
It was a mechanical figure which often broke down in the early days of filming Star Wars.

1109
01:17:17,560 --> 01:17:22,060
Over the years, the characters evolved along with the size of the collection.

1110
01:17:22,061 --> 01:17:27,060
This is the way that Yoda looked in Empire Strikes Back, the original Yoda.

1111
01:17:27,061 --> 01:17:30,060
You can tell that he has changed over the years.

1112
01:17:30,061 --> 01:17:35,060
This is the Yoda up here from episode two. That's a licensed item.

1113
01:17:35,061 --> 01:17:39,060
Yoda, the fighting Jedi, becomes a lot fiercer.

1114
01:17:39,061 --> 01:17:42,060
When I was down in Australia, I had an action figure made of myself.

1115
01:17:42,061 --> 01:17:46,060
They asked me what figure I wanted the body on, and of course I said Obi-Wan.

1116
01:17:46,061 --> 01:17:49,060
Having the beard, that sort of worked pretty well.

1117
01:17:49,061 --> 01:17:54,360
In 1991, he finally got the chance to purchase something that couldn't be bought in his store.

1118
01:17:54,860 --> 01:17:57,620
But it would mean spending more than he ever imagined.

1119
01:17:58,920 --> 01:18:06,080
I had the opportunity to purchase some actual props from the producer of the first two Star Wars movies.

1120
01:18:06,540 --> 01:18:09,160
And that was a sizable expenditure.

1121
01:18:09,840 --> 01:18:13,640
And the only way I could do that was to get a second mortgage on my house.

1122
01:18:14,020 --> 01:18:17,100
And so I can still remember the guy who was selling it saying,

1123
01:18:17,101 --> 01:18:20,040
So which of these do you think you want to buy?

1124
01:18:20,940 --> 01:18:22,660
And I said, all of them.

1125
01:18:23,600 --> 01:18:28,200
As he continued to accumulate everything Star Wars, cost wasn't the only problem.

1126
01:18:28,920 --> 01:18:32,680
We kept adding floors on as the collection kept growing.

1127
01:18:34,100 --> 01:18:43,420
And even after I added on to the house, I ran out of space and I got one storage locker, which eventually became five storage lockers.

1128
01:18:43,520 --> 01:18:45,000
So space was a problem.

1129
01:18:45,001 --> 01:18:52,260
As the collection became so big, Steve had to hire a curator to organize, catalog, and continue searching for new items.

1130
01:18:52,880 --> 01:18:55,880
But Steve still holds the collection closest to his heart.

1131
01:18:56,240 --> 01:19:02,360
The question about whether you have a favorite piece is sort of like saying to the proud parents of eight children,

1132
01:19:02,820 --> 01:19:04,400
which is your favorite kit?

1133
01:19:05,100 --> 01:19:11,180
I love some of the props, but I also love things that fans have made.

1134
01:19:11,181 --> 01:19:19,100
There's a friend of mine in Mexico City who makes out of tin plate Star Wars models.

1135
01:19:19,680 --> 01:19:22,080
And he makes one for himself and one for me.

1136
01:19:22,960 --> 01:19:27,220
